1
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:19,370
[Girl Humming]

2
00:00:19,430 --> 00:00:24,370
[Humming Continues]

3
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:29,710
[Girl Sighs]

4
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:36,380
[Continues Humming]

5
00:00:46,790 --> 00:00:50,730
[Humming Continues]

6
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,730
[Ticking]

7
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,400
[Humming Continues]

8
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:00,500
[GirlSighs]

9
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:09,010
[Humming Continues]

10
00:01:28,470 --> 00:01:30,530
[Girl]
Bing.

11
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:34,190
[Continues Humming]

12
00:02:34,270 --> 00:02:36,860
[Girl Murmurs]

13
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:41,370
[Girl Giggling]

14
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:43,880
[Girl Hums, Rips Paper]

15
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:00,980
[Birds Chirping]

16
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:09,660
[Woman Narrating]
Maycomb was a tired, old town,

17
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:13,830
even in 1932
when l first knew it.

18
00:03:13,910 --> 00:03:17,170
Some how it was hotter then.

19
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:20,840
Men's stiff collars wilted
by 9:00 in the morning.

20
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:25,680
Ladies bathed before noon,
after their 3:00 naps...

21
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:28,520
and by night fall
were like soft tea cakes...

22
00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:31,520
with frostings of sweat
and sweet talcum.

23
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:37,860
The day was 24 hours long,
but it seemed longer.

24
00:03:37,930 --> 00:03:40,200
There was no hurry,
for there was nowhere to go...

25
00:03:40,270 --> 00:03:43,360
and nothing to buy:;
no money to buy it with.

26
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:45,700
Although Maycomb County
had recently been told...

27
00:03:45,770 --> 00:03:49,710
that it had nothing to fear
but fear itself.

28
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,710
That summer
I was six years old.

29
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:56,550
- Good morning, Mr. Cunningham.
- Good morning, miss.

30
00:03:56,620 --> 00:04:00,310
My daddy's getting dressed.
Would you like me to call hI'm for you?

31
00:04:00,390 --> 00:04:04,320
- I don't care to bother.
- Why, it's no bother, Mr. Cunningham.

32
00:04:04,390 --> 00:04:07,090
He'll be happy to see you.
Atticus!

33
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:11,830
Atticus?

34
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,160
It's Mr. Cunningham.

35
00:04:15,570 --> 00:04:18,660
- Good morning, Walter.
- Good morning, Mr. Finch.

36
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:21,400
I didn't want to bother you none.

37
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:24,500
I brung you these here hickory nuts
as part of my entailment.

38
00:04:24,580 --> 00:04:28,340
Well, I thank you. The collards
we had last week were delicious.

39
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:30,350
Well, Morning.

40
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:32,350
Morning.

41
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:40,520
Scout, I think maybe, uh,
next time Mr. Cunningham comes,

42
00:04:40,590 --> 00:04:42,530
you better not call me.

43
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:45,690
- I thought you'd want to thank him.
- Oh, I do.

44
00:04:45,770 --> 00:04:49,360
I think it embarrasses him
to be thanked.

45
00:04:53,770 --> 00:04:56,210
Why does he bring you
all this stuff?

46
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:58,870
He's paying me for some legal work
I did for him.

47
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:00,970
Why does he pay you like this?

48
00:05:01,050 --> 00:05:03,740
That's the only way he can.
He has no money.

49
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:06,150
- Is he poor?
- Yes.

50
00:05:08,390 --> 00:05:10,480
Are we poor?

51
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:12,650
We are, indeed.

52
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:15,490
Are we as poor
as the Cunninghams?

53
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:17,660
No, not exactly.

54
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:20,670
Cunninghams are
country folks, farmers.

55
00:05:20,730 --> 00:05:24,500
- The crash hit them the hardest.
- [ Screen Door Squeaks ]

56
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:26,660
Scout, call your brother.

57
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:30,010
[ Scout ]
Atticus? Jem's up in the tree.

58
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:33,740
He says he won't come down
until you agree to play football
for the Methodists.

59
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:39,120
Jem?

60
00:05:42,090 --> 00:05:46,530
Son, why don't you come down out
of there now and have your breakfast.

61
00:05:46,590 --> 00:05:49,030
Calpurnia has a good one.
Hot biscuits.

62
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:52,360
[ Jem ] Not 'til you agree
to play football for the Methodists.

63
00:05:52,430 --> 00:05:57,370
Oh, son, I can't do that.
I'm too old to get out there.

64
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:00,300
After all,
I'm the only father you have.

65
00:06:00,370 --> 00:06:02,740
Wouldn't want me to get
my head knocked off, would you?

66
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:04,940
I ain't comin' down.

67
00:06:06,210 --> 00:06:08,480
Suit yourself.

68
00:06:11,850 --> 00:06:13,790
[ Woman ]
Good morning.

69
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:16,990
Good morning,
Miss Maudie.

70
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:22,020
- What's going on over there?
- I'm having a terrible time,
Miss Maudie.

71
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:25,760
Jem's stayin' up in the tree
until Atticus agrees to play
football for the Methodists.

72
00:06:25,830 --> 00:06:29,320
Atticus says
he's too old.

73
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:32,390
Every time I'm wantin'
to do something, he's too old.

74
00:06:32,470 --> 00:06:34,500
He's too old for anything!

75
00:06:34,570 --> 00:06:37,340
[ Maudie ]
He can do plenty of things.

76
00:06:37,410 --> 00:06:40,850
You be good, children.
Mind Cal.

77
00:06:40,910 --> 00:06:44,350
- Morning, Maudie.
- Morning, Atticus.

78
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:46,510
He won't let me have a gun,

79
00:06:46,590 --> 00:06:50,420
and he'll only play
touch football with me, never tackle.

80
00:06:50,490 --> 00:06:55,520
He can make somebody's will
so airtight, you can't break it.

81
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:59,690
You count your blessings
and stop complaining, both of you.

82
00:06:59,770 --> 00:07:02,860
Thank your stars
he has the sense to act his age.

83
00:07:02,940 --> 00:07:06,460
- Jem, he is pretty old.
- I can't help that.

84
00:07:10,110 --> 00:07:13,140
Hey.

85
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:17,740
Hey, yourself.

86
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:21,250
I'm Charles Baker Harris.
I can read.

87
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:24,810
You got anything needs readin',
I can do it.

88
00:07:24,890 --> 00:07:27,760
How old are you?
Four and a half?

89
00:07:27,830 --> 00:07:30,990
- Goin' on seven.
- No wonder then.

90
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:33,330
Scout's been readin'
since she was born,

91
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:36,330
and she don't start school
'til next month.

92
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:39,000
You look right puny
for goin' on seven.

93
00:07:39,070 --> 00:07:41,510
I'm little, but I'm old.

94
00:07:41,570 --> 00:07:44,170
Folks call me Dill.

95
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:46,340
I'm from Meridian, Mississippi.

96
00:07:46,410 --> 00:07:50,350
I'm spending two weeks next door
with my Aunt Stephanie.

97
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:53,080
My mama worked for a photographer
in Meridian.

98
00:07:53,150 --> 00:07:58,180
She entered my picture
in the "Beautiful Child" contest
and won five dollars on me.

99
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:03,630
She give the money to me, and I went
to the picture show 20 times with it.

100
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:07,500
- Our mama's dead, but we got a daddy.
- Where's your daddy?

101
00:08:07,570 --> 00:08:11,500
- I haven't got one.
- Is he dead?

102
00:08:11,570 --> 00:08:13,800
[ Dill ]
No.

103
00:08:13,870 --> 00:08:18,210
Well, if he's not dead,
you've got one, haven't you?

104
00:08:20,450 --> 00:08:23,470
- [ Screen Door Squeaks ]
- Hush, Scout.

105
00:08:25,050 --> 00:08:27,990
[ Softly ]
What did I do? What did I do?

106
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:32,660
Dill, this is Calpurnia.

107
00:08:32,730 --> 00:08:34,660
Pleased to know you, Dill.

108
00:08:34,730 --> 00:08:36,790
[ Dill ]
Pleased to know you.

109
00:08:36,860 --> 00:08:38,990
My daddy owns
the L&N Railroad.

110
00:08:39,070 --> 00:08:42,500
He's gonna let me run the engine
all the way to New Orleans.

111
00:08:42,570 --> 00:08:44,500
Is that so?

112
00:08:46,410 --> 00:08:49,000
- [ Dill ]
He says I can invite anybody--
- Shh.

113
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:53,340
[ Footsteps ]

114
00:08:53,410 --> 00:08:56,250
There goes the meanest man
that ever took a breath of life.

115
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:03,290
Why is he the meanest man?

116
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:06,620
Well, for one thing,

117
00:09:06,690 --> 00:09:09,290
he has a boy named Boo...

118
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:12,660
that he keeps chained to a bed
in the house over yonder.

119
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:16,930
Come on.

120
00:09:22,210 --> 00:09:24,800
See? He lives over there.

121
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:26,970
Boo only comes out
at night...

122
00:09:27,050 --> 00:09:30,310
when you're asleep
and it's pitch dark.

123
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:32,540
When you wake up at night,
you can hear him.

124
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:35,320
Once I heard hI'm scratching
on our screen door,

125
00:09:35,390 --> 00:09:38,790
but he was gone
by the time Atticus got there.

126
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:42,190
Wonder what he does in there.

127
00:09:42,260 --> 00:09:45,200
Wonder what he looks like.

128
00:09:45,270 --> 00:09:49,000
Well...
judging from his tracks,

129
00:09:49,070 --> 00:09:51,700
he's about
six and a half feet tall.

130
00:09:51,770 --> 00:09:56,040
He eats raw squirrels
and all the cats he can catch.

131
00:09:56,110 --> 00:10:00,170
There's a long, jagged scar
that runs across his face.

132
00:10:00,250 --> 00:10:02,240
His teeth are yellow and rotten.

133
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:06,510
His eyes are popped,
and he drools most of the time.

134
00:10:07,750 --> 00:10:10,280
Oh, I don't believe you.

135
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:12,880
Dill, what are you doing here?

136
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:15,450
My Lord, Aunt Stephanie!
You almost gave me a heart attack!

137
00:10:15,530 --> 00:10:17,860
Dill, I don't want you playing
around that house over there.

138
00:10:17,930 --> 00:10:20,230
A maniac lives there,
and he's dangerous.

139
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:24,700
See? I was trying to warn him
about Boo, but he wouldn't believe me.

140
00:10:24,770 --> 00:10:27,040
You better believe him,
Mr. Dill Harris.

141
00:10:27,110 --> 00:10:29,270
Tell hI'm about the time Boo
tried to kill his papa.

142
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:33,710
I was standing in my yard
when his mama come out yelling,
"He's killing us all!"

143
00:10:33,780 --> 00:10:37,550
Turned out that Boo was sitting
in the living room cutting up
the paper for his scrapbook,

144
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:40,210
and when his daddy come by,
he reached over with his scissors,

145
00:10:40,290 --> 00:10:43,620
stabbed hI'm in his leg, pulled them out
and went right on cutting the paper.

146
00:10:43,690 --> 00:10:45,660
They wanted to send him
to an asylum,

147
00:10:45,730 --> 00:10:48,990
but his daddy said,
"No Radley is going to any asylum."

148
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:51,220
So they locked hI'm up
in the basement of the courthouse...

149
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:53,230
'til he nearly died of the damp...

150
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:55,270
and his daddy
brought hI'm back home.

151
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:59,200
There he is to this day,
sitting over there with his scissors.

152
00:10:59,270 --> 00:11:02,000
Lord knows what
he's doing or thinking.

153
00:11:04,410 --> 00:11:04,740
[ Shouting ]
Six, seven, eight,

154
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:08,340
[ Shouting ]
Six, seven, eight,

155
00:11:08,410 --> 00:11:12,180
- [ Bell Tolling ]
- nine, ten!

156
00:11:12,250 --> 00:11:15,850
[ Tolling Continues ]

157
00:11:15,920 --> 00:11:18,520
Come on, Scout!
It's 5:00!

158
00:11:22,260 --> 00:11:25,700
- Where you going?
- Time to meet Atticus.

159
00:11:25,770 --> 00:11:28,700
- Why do you call your daddy Atticus?
- 'Cause Jem does.

160
00:11:28,770 --> 00:11:31,200
- Why does he?
- I don't know.

161
00:11:31,270 --> 00:11:34,100
He just started to
ever since he began talking.

162
00:11:34,170 --> 00:11:36,110
[ Spits ]

163
00:11:38,010 --> 00:11:40,210
Wait. Stop.

164
00:11:40,280 --> 00:11:42,540
- Miss Dubose is on her porch.
- [ Groans ]

165
00:11:42,620 --> 00:11:46,210
Listen, no matter what she says to you,
don't answer her back.

166
00:11:46,290 --> 00:11:49,050
There's a Confederate pistol
in her lap under her shawl,

167
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:51,390
and she'll kill you
quick as look at you.

168
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:53,390
Come on.

169
00:12:01,230 --> 00:12:04,830
- Hey, Miss Dubose.
- Don't you say "hey" to me,
you ugly girl!

170
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:08,360
You say,
"Good afternoon, Miss Dubose."

171
00:12:08,440 --> 00:12:11,000
You come over here
when I'm talking to you.

172
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:14,170
- You come over here, I said!
- Hi, Atticus.

173
00:12:14,250 --> 00:12:17,510
- [ Dubose Continues Shouting ]
- Atticus, this is Dill.
He's Miss Stephanie's nephew.

174
00:12:17,580 --> 00:12:19,520
[ Dubose ] Don't your daddy
teach you to respect old people?

175
00:12:19,590 --> 00:12:22,710
You come back here,
Jean Louise Finch!

176
00:12:22,790 --> 00:12:26,190
Good afternoon, Miss Dubose.

177
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:30,460
My, you look like
a picture this afternoon.

178
00:12:30,530 --> 00:12:32,930
[ Scout Whispers ]
He don't say a picture of what.

179
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,520
My goodness gracious,
look at your flowers.

180
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:38,540
Have you ever seen
anything more beautiful?

181
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:43,060
Miss Dubose,
the gardens at Bellingrath
have nothing to compare...

182
00:12:43,140 --> 00:12:45,200
with your flowers.

183
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:48,210
I don't think they're
as nice as last year.

184
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:51,880
He gets her interested in something nice
so she forgets to be mean.

185
00:12:51,950 --> 00:12:55,050
Your yard is going to be
the showplace of this town.

186
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:59,060
Well, grand seeing you,
Miss Dubose.

187
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:14,170
"l had two cats...

188
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:17,180
which I brought ashore...

189
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:20,680
on my first raft.

190
00:13:20,750 --> 00:13:24,310
And I had a dog."

191
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:31,680
Atticus, do you think Boo Radley
ever really comes and looks
in my window at night?

192
00:13:31,760 --> 00:13:37,350
- Jem says he does. This afternoon
when we were over by their house--
- Scout...

193
00:13:37,430 --> 00:13:41,870
I told you and Jem
to leave those poor people alone.

194
00:13:41,930 --> 00:13:44,030
I want you to stay away
from their house...

195
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:45,760
and stop tormenting them.

196
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:47,360
Yes, sir.

197
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:50,380
That's all the reading
for tonight, honey. It's getting late.

198
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:54,210
- What time is it?
- 8:30.

199
00:13:54,280 --> 00:13:56,250
May I see your watch?

200
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:18,330
"To Atticus,
my beloved husband."

201
00:14:19,910 --> 00:14:23,340
Atticus, Jem says this watch
is gonna belong to hI'm someday.

202
00:14:23,410 --> 00:14:26,570
- That's right.
- Why?

203
00:14:26,650 --> 00:14:30,670
Well...
it's customary...

204
00:14:30,750 --> 00:14:33,010
for the boy to have
his father's watch.

205
00:14:33,090 --> 00:14:36,540
What are you gonna give me?

206
00:14:40,090 --> 00:14:44,860
I don't know that I have
much else of value that belongs to me.

207
00:14:47,270 --> 00:14:49,860
But there's a pearl necklace,

208
00:14:49,940 --> 00:14:53,370
there's a ring that
belonged to your mother.

209
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:55,430
I put them away,

210
00:14:55,510 --> 00:14:58,140
and they're to be yours.

211
00:15:07,220 --> 00:15:09,480
- Good night, Scout.
- Good night.

212
00:15:19,570 --> 00:15:21,830
- [ Atticus ] Good night, Jem.
- [ Jem ] Good night.

213
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:38,180
- Jem?
- [ Jem ] Yes?

214
00:15:40,590 --> 00:15:45,850
- [ Sighs ]
How old was I when Mama died?
- Two.

215
00:15:45,930 --> 00:15:49,690
- How old were you?
- Six.

216
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:53,200
- Old as I am now?
- Mm-hmm.

217
00:15:55,430 --> 00:15:59,370
- Was Mama pretty?
- Mm-hmm.

218
00:15:59,440 --> 00:16:02,640
[ Scout ]
Was Mama nice?

219
00:16:02,710 --> 00:16:05,800
[ Jem ]
Mm-hmm.

220
00:16:05,880 --> 00:16:09,640
- Did you love her?
- Yes.

221
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:13,980
- Did I love her?
- Mm-hmm.

222
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:19,990
- Do you miss her?
- Mm-hmm.

223
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:29,160
[ Footsteps Approaching ]

224
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:33,230
- [ Man ] Evening, Atticus.
- Good evening, Judge.

225
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:38,270
Rather warm, isn't it?

226
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:40,340
Yes, indeed.

227
00:16:45,580 --> 00:16:47,520
How's Mrs. Taylor?

228
00:16:47,590 --> 00:16:50,180
She's fine.
Fine, thank you.

229
00:16:54,090 --> 00:16:57,060
Atticus, you've heard
about Tom Robinson.

230
00:16:58,260 --> 00:17:00,130
Yes, sir.

231
00:17:00,200 --> 00:17:04,690
Grand jury will get around
to charging hI'm tomorrow.

232
00:17:09,380 --> 00:17:13,610
I was thinking about appointing you
to take his case.

233
00:17:17,380 --> 00:17:21,980
Now I realize you're very busy
these days with your practice.

234
00:17:22,050 --> 00:17:26,150
And your children need
a great deal of your time.

235
00:17:27,890 --> 00:17:30,330
Yes, sir.

236
00:17:35,070 --> 00:17:37,130
I'll take the case.

237
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:42,300
I'll send a boy over for you tomorrow
when his hearing comes up.

238
00:17:45,240 --> 00:17:48,680
Well... I'll see you tomorrow,
Atticus.

239
00:17:48,750 --> 00:17:50,680
Yes, sir.

240
00:17:53,250 --> 00:17:55,690
And thank you.

241
00:17:55,760 --> 00:17:57,690
Yes, sir.

242
00:18:07,700 --> 00:18:12,800
Hey, Jem, I bet you a "Grey Ghost"
against two "Tom Swifts"...

243
00:18:12,870 --> 00:18:17,970
you wouldn't go any farther
than Boo Radley's gate.

244
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:20,410
Aw.

245
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:23,810
- Scared to, ain't you?
- I ain't scared.

246
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:27,480
I go past Boo Radley's house
nearly every day of my life.

247
00:18:27,550 --> 00:18:30,820
- Always running.
- You hush up, Scout.

248
00:18:34,960 --> 00:18:37,990
- Come on, Dill!
- Me first! Me first!

249
00:18:38,060 --> 00:18:40,660
- Me first! Me first!
- You gotta let Dill be first.

250
00:18:40,730 --> 00:18:44,000
- No! Me, me, me!
- [ Dill ] Oh, let her be first.

251
00:18:44,070 --> 00:18:46,090
All right, get in!

252
00:18:46,170 --> 00:18:49,340
- Hurry up!
- All right.

253
00:18:49,410 --> 00:18:53,000
- You ready?
- Uh-huh. Let her go.

254
00:19:07,890 --> 00:19:10,730
Scout!

255
00:19:14,270 --> 00:19:17,600
Scout, get away from there!
Scout, come on!

256
00:19:17,670 --> 00:19:21,070
Don't just lie there!
Get up!

257
00:19:24,940 --> 00:19:26,970
Come on, Scout.

258
00:19:34,090 --> 00:19:36,490
Run for your life, Scout!

259
00:19:36,560 --> 00:19:38,820
Come on, Dill!

260
00:19:45,060 --> 00:19:48,000
[ Panting ]
Now who's a coward?

261
00:19:48,070 --> 00:19:53,370
You tell them about this back
in Meridian County, Mr. Dill Harris.

262
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:55,670
I'll tell you what let's do.

263
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:59,680
Let's go down to the courthouse and see
the room that they locked Boo up in.

264
00:19:59,750 --> 00:20:03,180
My aunt says it's bat-infested,
and he nearly died from the mildew.

265
00:20:03,250 --> 00:20:07,850
Come on! I bet they got chains
and instruments of torture down there.

266
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:09,850
Come on!

267
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:14,850
- Jem Finch?
- Yes, sir?

268
00:20:14,930 --> 00:20:18,520
If you're looking for your daddy,
he's inside the courthouse.

269
00:20:18,600 --> 00:20:21,860
-Thank you, sir, but we're not looking--
-Thank you, Mr. Townsend.

270
00:20:23,770 --> 00:20:26,360
What's your daddy doing
in the courthouse?

271
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:29,370
He's a lawyer,
and he has a case.

272
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:31,880
The grand jury is charging
his client today.

273
00:20:31,940 --> 00:20:34,880
I heard something about it last night
when Judge Taylor came over.

274
00:20:34,950 --> 00:20:38,040
- Let's go watch.
- No, Dill.

275
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:41,110
- He wouldn't like that. Dill!
- Dill!

276
00:20:43,760 --> 00:20:45,880
[ Jem, Hushed ]
Dill, wait a minute.

277
00:20:47,790 --> 00:20:49,730
Shh.

278
00:20:49,800 --> 00:20:51,890
- [ Typing ]
- Is that the courtroom?

279
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:54,730
[ Whispers ]
Yeah. Shh.

280
00:20:58,470 --> 00:21:01,170
- I can't see anything.
- Shh.

281
00:21:02,740 --> 00:21:05,180
Y'all lift me up
so I can see what's going on.

282
00:21:05,240 --> 00:21:08,340
- All right.
- Make a saddle, Scout.

283
00:21:12,750 --> 00:21:15,850
Not much is happenin'.

284
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:19,150
The judge looks like
he's asleep.

285
00:21:19,220 --> 00:21:21,520
[ Groaning ]

286
00:21:21,590 --> 00:21:24,530
I see your daddy
and a colored man.

287
00:21:24,600 --> 00:21:27,500
- The colored man--
- Shh, shh.

288
00:21:27,570 --> 00:21:30,190
The colored man looks to me
like he's cryin'.

289
00:21:30,270 --> 00:21:34,000
- [ Man ] I seen hI'm with Mayella.
- I wonder what he's done
to cry about?

290
00:21:34,070 --> 00:21:35,800
What's going on?

291
00:21:35,870 --> 00:21:40,710
There's a whole lot of men
sittin' together on one side,

292
00:21:40,780 --> 00:21:44,770
and one man keeps pointin'
at the colored man and yellin'.

293
00:21:44,850 --> 00:21:47,550
They're taking
the colored man away.

294
00:21:47,620 --> 00:21:50,420
- Where's Atticus?
- [ Phone Ringing ]

295
00:21:50,490 --> 00:21:54,890
[ Dill ]
I can't see your daddy now either.

296
00:21:56,960 --> 00:21:59,830
- I wonder where in the world--
- Scout, Jem.

297
00:22:01,600 --> 00:22:04,160
What in the world
are you doing here?

298
00:22:04,240 --> 00:22:06,670
[ Jem ]
Hello, Atticus.

299
00:22:06,740 --> 00:22:08,830
What are you doing here?

300
00:22:08,910 --> 00:22:12,500
We came down to find out where
Boo Radley was locked up.

301
00:22:12,580 --> 00:22:15,510
We wanted to see the bats.

302
00:22:15,580 --> 00:22:19,020
I want you all
back home right away.

303
00:22:19,080 --> 00:22:21,520
- Yes, sir.
- Run along now.

304
00:22:21,590 --> 00:22:24,180
I'll see you there for dinner.

305
00:22:34,930 --> 00:22:37,960
Hey, howdy, Cap'n.

306
00:22:38,040 --> 00:22:40,370
Mr. Ewell.

307
00:22:40,440 --> 00:22:43,200
Cap'n, I'm real sorry
they picked you...

308
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:46,040
to defend that nigger
that raped my Mayella.

309
00:22:46,110 --> 00:22:49,880
I don't know why I didn't kill him
myself instead of going to the sheriff.

310
00:22:49,950 --> 00:22:53,280
I'd have saved you and the sheriff
and the taxpayers lots of trouble.

311
00:22:53,350 --> 00:22:55,050
Excuse me. I'm busy.

312
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:59,990
Hey, Cap'n, somebody told me
just now that they thought...

313
00:23:00,060 --> 00:23:05,160
that you believed Tom Robinson's story
agin ourn.

314
00:23:05,230 --> 00:23:07,490
You know what I said?

315
00:23:07,570 --> 00:23:11,660
[ Laughs ] I said,
"You wrong, man. You dead wrong!

316
00:23:11,740 --> 00:23:15,670
Mr. Finch ain't taking
his story against ourn."

317
00:23:15,740 --> 00:23:17,680
Well, they was wrong,
wasn't they?

318
00:23:17,740 --> 00:23:20,180
I've been appointed
to defend Tom Robinson.

319
00:23:20,250 --> 00:23:23,840
Now that he's been charged,
that's what I intend to do.

320
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:27,180
- You taking his story--
- If you'll excuse me, Mr. Ewell.

321
00:23:31,590 --> 00:23:34,680
What kind of man are you?

322
00:23:34,760 --> 00:23:37,350
You got children of your own.

323
00:23:39,260 --> 00:23:42,860
[ Radio ]

324
00:23:42,940 --> 00:23:46,630
[ Dill Whispering ]
Hey, Jem! Jem?

325
00:23:56,620 --> 00:23:59,710
I think we ought to stay right here
in Miss Stephanie's yard.

326
00:23:59,790 --> 00:24:02,980
You don't have to come along...
Angel May.

327
00:24:17,740 --> 00:24:21,000
What are you gonna do?

328
00:24:21,070 --> 00:24:23,670
[ Hushed ] Gonna look in a window
at the Radley house...

329
00:24:23,740 --> 00:24:26,840
and see if we can get
a look at Boo Radley!

330
00:24:26,910 --> 00:24:29,240
Come on, Dill.

331
00:24:32,750 --> 00:24:36,020
- Jem, please, I'm scared.
- Go home if you're scared!

332
00:24:36,090 --> 00:24:38,610
I swear, Scout, you act
more like a girl all the time.

333
00:24:38,690 --> 00:24:40,850
- Come on, Dill.
- Wait for me. I'm coming.

334
00:24:40,930 --> 00:24:42,860
Shh.

335
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:49,530
We'll go around back...

336
00:24:49,600 --> 00:24:53,700
and crawl under the fence
at the rear of the lot.

337
00:24:53,770 --> 00:24:59,370
- I don't believe we can
be seen from there.
- [ Thud, Squeaking ]

338
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:05,280
[ Squeaking Continues ]

339
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:08,650
Come on.

340
00:25:22,570 --> 00:25:24,500
[ Whispers ]
Come on.

341
00:25:26,570 --> 00:25:28,510
Come on.

342
00:25:32,910 --> 00:25:34,850
Hold it up for me.

343
00:25:38,920 --> 00:25:40,850
Shh.

344
00:25:51,930 --> 00:25:53,990
Don't make a sound.

345
00:25:55,930 --> 00:25:58,200
[ Gate Squeaks ]

346
00:26:01,640 --> 00:26:03,570
Spit on it.

347
00:26:03,640 --> 00:26:07,100
[ Spitting ]

348
00:26:07,180 --> 00:26:09,150
All right.

349
00:26:12,220 --> 00:26:14,310
[ Gate Squeaks ]

350
00:26:14,390 --> 00:26:17,690
- [ Scout ] Jem.
- Shh. Spit some more.

351
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:19,850
[ Spitting ]

352
00:26:19,930 --> 00:26:22,490
[ Spitting Continues ]

353
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:24,500
All right.

354
00:26:45,950 --> 00:26:47,890
Come on.

355
00:27:05,640 --> 00:27:07,570
[ Step Creaks ]

356
00:27:17,720 --> 00:27:19,650
[ Porch Creaks ]

357
00:28:11,370 --> 00:28:14,130
[ Gasping ]

358
00:28:17,910 --> 00:28:20,140
[ Whimpers ]

359
00:28:40,870 --> 00:28:42,800
Hurry. Hurry.

360
00:28:44,570 --> 00:28:46,500
Hurry!

361
00:28:52,580 --> 00:28:54,550
[ Material Rips ]

362
00:28:54,610 --> 00:28:57,280
[ Groaning ]

363
00:28:57,350 --> 00:29:01,950
[ Whispering ]
Scout! Scout!

364
00:29:17,170 --> 00:29:21,130
[ Dog Barking ]

365
00:29:21,210 --> 00:29:23,800
[ Barking Continues ]

366
00:29:29,650 --> 00:29:31,640
Quick. Come on.

367
00:29:33,890 --> 00:29:37,150
[ All Panting,
Dill Coughing ]

368
00:29:37,220 --> 00:29:39,320
- Shh!
- [ Continues Coughing ]

369
00:29:39,390 --> 00:29:42,720
- Quiet.
- Shh.

370
00:29:42,790 --> 00:29:44,990
[ Panting ]

371
00:29:45,060 --> 00:29:48,330
- What are you gonna do for pants, Jem?
- I don't know.

372
00:29:48,400 --> 00:29:51,160
- [ Continues Panting ]
- [ Screen Door Squeaks ]

373
00:29:51,240 --> 00:29:54,500
[ Aunt Stephanie ]
Dill? Dill?

374
00:29:55,570 --> 00:29:57,840
You come on in now.

375
00:29:57,910 --> 00:30:01,000
- Shh.
- I better go.

376
00:30:01,080 --> 00:30:04,670
- [ Aunt Stephanie ] Dill?
- Coming, Aunt Stephanie!

377
00:30:04,750 --> 00:30:07,510
- [ Screen Door Squeaks ]
- So long. I'll see you next summer.

378
00:30:07,590 --> 00:30:09,490
- So long.
- So long.

379
00:30:09,550 --> 00:30:12,610
- [ Aunt Stephanie ] Dill!
- I'm comin'!

380
00:30:12,690 --> 00:30:14,680
I'm going back
after my pants.

381
00:30:14,760 --> 00:30:17,190
Please, Jem.
Come on in the house.

382
00:30:17,260 --> 00:30:19,860
I can't go in
without my pants.

383
00:30:19,930 --> 00:30:22,870
- Then I'm going to call Atticus.
- No, you're not.

384
00:30:22,930 --> 00:30:26,200
Now, listen. Atticus ain't never
whipped me since I can remember,

385
00:30:26,270 --> 00:30:29,040
and I plan to keep it that way.

386
00:30:29,110 --> 00:30:33,200
- Then I'm going with you.
- You ain't. You stay right here.

387
00:30:33,280 --> 00:30:35,870
I'll be back
before you can count to ten.

388
00:30:38,120 --> 00:30:40,050
Jem.

389
00:30:45,960 --> 00:30:49,150
One. Two.

390
00:30:49,230 --> 00:30:51,390
Three.

391
00:30:51,460 --> 00:30:53,520
- Four.
- [ Atticus ] Jem?

392
00:30:55,300 --> 00:30:57,390
Scout?

393
00:30:57,470 --> 00:30:59,730
Come on in.

394
00:30:59,800 --> 00:31:01,970
- [ Screen Door Squeaks, Door Closes ]
- Five.

395
00:31:02,040 --> 00:31:03,970
Six.

396
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:07,070
Seven.
Eight.

397
00:31:07,150 --> 00:31:09,510
Nine.

398
00:31:09,580 --> 00:31:11,520
Ten.

399
00:31:12,780 --> 00:31:14,840
Eleven.

400
00:31:14,920 --> 00:31:17,010
Twelve.

401
00:31:17,090 --> 00:31:19,180
Thirteen.

402
00:31:19,260 --> 00:31:21,730
- Fourteen.
- [ Gunshot ]

403
00:31:21,790 --> 00:31:24,190
[ Dog Barking ]

404
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:27,660
[ Clattering,
Barking Continues ]

405
00:31:27,730 --> 00:31:29,860
Jem!

406
00:31:31,070 --> 00:31:35,200
- [ Door Opens, Closes ]
- Shh.

407
00:31:35,270 --> 00:31:41,040
[ Barking Continues ]

408
00:31:41,110 --> 00:31:44,170
[ Aunt Stephanie Yelling ]
What was that? What is it?

409
00:31:44,250 --> 00:31:47,880
What happened?
What happened?

410
00:31:47,950 --> 00:31:50,550
What's goin' on?
What is it?

411
00:31:50,620 --> 00:31:53,060
Atticus, what is it?

412
00:31:53,120 --> 00:31:56,890
Will somebody please tell me
what's goin' on?

413
00:31:56,960 --> 00:31:59,560
Uh, Mr. Radley...

414
00:31:59,630 --> 00:32:02,660
shot at a prowler out
in his collard patch.

415
00:32:02,730 --> 00:32:04,830
A prowler?
Oh, Maudie!

416
00:32:04,900 --> 00:32:07,670
Whoever it was
won't be back anytime soon.

417
00:32:07,740 --> 00:32:09,670
Mr. Radley must have scared them
out of their wits.

418
00:32:09,740 --> 00:32:12,010
- Good night. Good night.
- Good night, Atticus.

419
00:32:12,080 --> 00:32:14,810
Oh, scared the living daylights
out of me.

420
00:32:14,880 --> 00:32:18,340
A prowler?
He said a prowler?

421
00:32:19,750 --> 00:32:22,190
Come on now.
The excitement's over.

422
00:32:22,250 --> 00:32:24,190
Time for bed.

423
00:32:25,420 --> 00:32:27,690
Scout. Jem.

424
00:32:45,910 --> 00:32:48,540
[ Maudie ]
Morning.

425
00:32:48,610 --> 00:32:50,550
Good morning,
Miss Maudie.

426
00:32:50,620 --> 00:32:52,550
- Morning, Calpurnia.
- Morning, Maudie.

427
00:32:52,620 --> 00:32:54,710
Came to see if Jean Louise is ready
for her first day of school.

428
00:32:54,790 --> 00:32:56,720
- [ Cal ] Scout!
- Good morning, Miss Maudie.

429
00:32:56,790 --> 00:32:59,720
- You ready too?
- Yes, ma'am.
- Scout!

430
00:32:59,790 --> 00:33:03,090
What are you gonna do this morning
with both children at school?

431
00:33:03,160 --> 00:33:06,650
I don't know,
and that's the truth.

432
00:33:06,730 --> 00:33:09,330
I was thinking
about that just now.

433
00:33:09,400 --> 00:33:11,340
Scout!

434
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:16,500
Scout? Scout!

435
00:33:16,570 --> 00:33:18,670
Did you hear me, Scout?

436
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:20,680
Now hurry.

437
00:33:30,420 --> 00:33:32,390
[ Jem Laughs ]
Hey, look at Scout!

438
00:33:32,460 --> 00:33:34,020
Shh.

439
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:42,360
- Come on in here, Scout.
- [ Stifling Laughter ]

440
00:33:44,770 --> 00:33:47,700
Have your breakfast.

441
00:33:55,450 --> 00:33:58,380
I think your dress
is mighty becomin', honey.

442
00:33:58,450 --> 00:34:01,150
[ Cal ] Don't go tugging
at that dress, Scout.

443
00:34:01,220 --> 00:34:05,160
You want to have it all wrinkled
before you even get to school?

444
00:34:05,220 --> 00:34:09,750
I still don't see why I have
to wear a darn old dress.

445
00:34:09,830 --> 00:34:11,990
- You'll get used to it.
- I'm ready!

446
00:34:12,060 --> 00:34:15,500
Oh, Jem. Jem.

447
00:34:15,570 --> 00:34:19,660
It's half an hour
before school starts.

448
00:34:19,740 --> 00:34:23,170
Sit right down
and wait for your sister.

449
00:34:26,750 --> 00:34:31,380
- Hurry up, Scout.
- I'm  trying to.

450
00:34:31,450 --> 00:34:35,350
[ Jem ] Come on! It's your first day.
You want to be late?

451
00:34:37,260 --> 00:34:40,520
- I'm ready.
- [ Jem ] Let's go!

452
00:34:43,930 --> 00:34:45,860
Bye.

453
00:34:48,270 --> 00:34:50,200
Bye, everybody!

454
00:34:51,940 --> 00:34:54,200
[ Chuckling ]

455
00:34:54,270 --> 00:34:57,370
[ Shouting ]

456
00:35:05,380 --> 00:35:07,820
Darn you,
Walter Cunningham!

457
00:35:07,890 --> 00:35:12,450
[ Children Shouting ]

458
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:15,650
- [ Children ] Come on, Walter!
- Come on, Walter!

459
00:35:15,730 --> 00:35:17,660
- [ Jem ] Scout!
- Come on, Walter!

460
00:35:17,730 --> 00:35:19,660
Cut that out!

461
00:35:19,730 --> 00:35:21,820
What do you think you're doing?

462
00:35:21,900 --> 00:35:24,840
[ Grunting ]

463
00:35:24,900 --> 00:35:27,500
He made me start off
on the wrong foot!

464
00:35:27,570 --> 00:35:30,010
I was trying to explain
to that darn lady teacher...

465
00:35:30,070 --> 00:35:35,010
why he didn't have no money
for his lunch, and she got sore at me!

466
00:35:35,080 --> 00:35:39,070
Stop it! Stop it!

467
00:35:46,190 --> 00:35:50,360
Is your daddy Mr. Walter Cunningham
from Old Sarum?

468
00:35:52,260 --> 00:35:54,360
Come home and have dinner
with us, Walter.

469
00:35:54,430 --> 00:35:56,530
We'd be glad to have you.

470
00:35:58,100 --> 00:36:01,300
Our daddy is a friend
of your daddy's.

471
00:36:01,370 --> 00:36:03,310
[ Sighs ]

472
00:36:03,370 --> 00:36:06,470
Scout here is crazy.
She won't fight you no more.

473
00:36:10,050 --> 00:36:12,980
[ Atticus ]
I hope that's a dinner that you enjoy.

474
00:36:13,050 --> 00:36:16,710
Yes, sir. I don't know
when I've had a roast.

475
00:36:16,790 --> 00:36:21,320
We've been having squirrels
and rabbits lately.

476
00:36:21,390 --> 00:36:26,160
My pa and I go hunting
in our spare time.

477
00:36:26,230 --> 00:36:29,830
- You got a gun of your own?
- [ Walter ] Uh-huh.

478
00:36:29,900 --> 00:36:33,390
- [ Jem ] How long you had a gun?
- Oh, a year or so.

479
00:36:35,710 --> 00:36:38,510
- Can I have the syrup, please?
- Certainly, son.

480
00:36:38,580 --> 00:36:42,030
Cal, will you bring in
the syrup dish, please?

481
00:36:42,110 --> 00:36:44,050
Yes, sir.

482
00:36:44,120 --> 00:36:46,380
How old were you when you got
your first gun, Atticus?

483
00:36:46,450 --> 00:36:49,680
Thirteen or fourteen.

484
00:36:51,920 --> 00:36:55,550
I remember when my daddy
gave me that gun.

485
00:36:55,630 --> 00:36:59,530
He told me that I should never point
at anything in the house...

486
00:36:59,600 --> 00:37:04,430
and that he'd rather
I shoot at tin cans in the backyard.

487
00:37:04,500 --> 00:37:06,800
But he said that sooner
or later he supposed...

488
00:37:06,870 --> 00:37:11,310
the temptation to go after birds
would be too much...

489
00:37:11,380 --> 00:37:16,970
and that I could shoot all the blue jays
I wanted, if I could hit 'em;

490
00:37:17,050 --> 00:37:20,450
but to remember it was a sin
to kill a mockingbird.

491
00:37:20,520 --> 00:37:24,550
- Why?
- [ Atticus ] Well, I reckon...

492
00:37:24,620 --> 00:37:26,650
because mockingbirds
don't do anything...

493
00:37:26,720 --> 00:37:30,850
but make music
for us to enjoy.

494
00:37:30,930 --> 00:37:33,490
Don't eat people's gardens.

495
00:37:33,560 --> 00:37:36,830
Don't nest in the corn cribs.

496
00:37:36,900 --> 00:37:41,340
They don't do one thing but just
sing their hearts out for us.

497
00:37:42,640 --> 00:37:45,340
How'd you like school, Scout?

498
00:37:45,410 --> 00:37:48,350
All right.

499
00:37:48,410 --> 00:37:51,180
Oh, thank you, Cal.
That's for Walter.

500
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:15,100
- What in the Sam Hill
are you doing, Wa--
- [ Tapping ]

501
00:38:15,170 --> 00:38:20,870
But, Atticus, he's gone
and drowned his dinner in syrup,

502
00:38:20,950 --> 00:38:23,440
- and now he's pouring it all over--
- [ Cal ] Scout.

503
00:38:23,510 --> 00:38:26,920
- What?
- Come out here. I want to talk to you.

504
00:38:35,760 --> 00:38:37,690
That boy is your company.

505
00:38:37,760 --> 00:38:41,360
If he wants to eat that tablecloth,
you'll let him.

506
00:38:41,430 --> 00:38:43,530
If you can't act fit
to eat like folks,

507
00:38:43,600 --> 00:38:46,200
you can just sit here
and eat in the kitchen.

508
00:38:49,070 --> 00:38:51,040
[ Atticus ]
Scout.

509
00:38:56,050 --> 00:38:58,040
Scout.

510
00:39:03,220 --> 00:39:07,060
- [ Mumbling Softly ]
- Scout, what in the world
has got into you?

511
00:39:07,130 --> 00:39:10,220
- [ Repeating ] I'm not going back.
- Now, now.

512
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:16,420
Atticus, I'm not going back
to school anymore.

513
00:39:16,500 --> 00:39:18,900
Now, Scout,
it's just the first day.

514
00:39:18,970 --> 00:39:22,240
I don't care.
Everything went wrong.

515
00:39:22,310 --> 00:39:24,900
The teacher got mad
as the devil at me...

516
00:39:24,980 --> 00:39:29,240
and said you were teaching me
to read all wrong and to stop it,

517
00:39:29,310 --> 00:39:33,220
then acted like a fool and tried to give
Walter Cunningham a quarter...

518
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:37,850
when everybody knows Cunninghams
won't take nothin' from nobody.

519
00:39:37,920 --> 00:39:40,120
Any fool could have told her that.

520
00:39:40,190 --> 00:39:42,680
Well... maybe she's just nervous.

521
00:39:42,760 --> 00:39:46,860
After all, it's her first day
teaching school and being new here.

522
00:39:46,930 --> 00:39:51,200
- Oh, Atticus--
- Now, wait a minute.

523
00:39:53,270 --> 00:39:55,540
If you learn
this single trick, Scout,

524
00:39:55,610 --> 00:39:58,700
you'll get along a lot better
with all kinds of folks.

525
00:39:58,780 --> 00:40:01,040
You never really
understand a person...

526
00:40:01,110 --> 00:40:04,480
until you consider things
from his point of view.

527
00:40:04,550 --> 00:40:06,040
Sir?

528
00:40:06,120 --> 00:40:10,380
Until you climb inside of his skin
and walk around in it.

529
00:40:10,460 --> 00:40:15,720
But if I keep going to school,
we can't ever read anymore.

530
00:40:18,800 --> 00:40:21,390
Scout...

531
00:40:21,470 --> 00:40:25,230
do you know what
a compromise is?

532
00:40:25,300 --> 00:40:28,070
Bending the law?

533
00:40:29,810 --> 00:40:32,500
Uh... no.

534
00:40:32,580 --> 00:40:36,670
It's an agreement reached
by mutual consent.

535
00:40:36,750 --> 00:40:40,980
Now... here's
the way it works.

536
00:40:43,750 --> 00:40:48,690
You concede the necessity
of going to school,

537
00:40:48,760 --> 00:40:52,700
we'll keep right on reading
the same every night...

538
00:40:52,760 --> 00:40:55,530
just as we always have.

539
00:40:57,270 --> 00:40:59,200
That a bargain?

540
00:41:02,510 --> 00:41:05,340
[ Adult Scout Narrating ]
There just didn't seem to be...

541
00:41:05,410 --> 00:41:09,540
anyone or anything
Atticus couldn't explain.

542
00:41:09,610 --> 00:41:13,380
Though it wasn't a talent
that would arouse the admiration
of any of our friends,

543
00:41:13,450 --> 00:41:16,550
Jem and I had to admit
he was very good at that.

544
00:41:16,620 --> 00:41:21,720
- [ Dog Growling ]
- But that was all
he was good at... we thought.

545
00:41:21,790 --> 00:41:25,130
- See? There he is.
- [ Growling Continues ]

546
00:41:25,200 --> 00:41:27,690
[ Snarling, Growling ]

547
00:41:27,770 --> 00:41:30,700
[ Snarling, Growling Continue ]

548
00:41:34,310 --> 00:41:37,330
Scout, Jem,
come on inside.

549
00:41:37,410 --> 00:41:40,340
- [ Snarling Continues ]
- Come on! Come on. Get in.

550
00:41:48,590 --> 00:41:50,820
Mr. Finch?
This is Cal.

551
00:41:50,890 --> 00:41:53,760
I swear to God, there's a mad dog
down the street a piece.

552
00:41:53,820 --> 00:41:55,760
He's coming this way!

553
00:41:55,830 --> 00:42:01,530
[ Car Approaching ]

554
00:42:03,270 --> 00:42:06,130
There he is.

555
00:42:09,170 --> 00:42:11,370
He's got it, all right,
Mr. Finch.

556
00:42:14,610 --> 00:42:17,380
Stay inside, son.
Keep them in there, Cal.

557
00:42:18,620 --> 00:42:21,020
He's within range, Heck.

558
00:42:21,090 --> 00:42:23,880
- [ Dog Snarling ]
- Take him, Mr. Finch.

559
00:42:23,960 --> 00:42:27,720
- Oh, no, Mr. Tate! He can't shoot!
- Don't waste time, Heck.

560
00:42:27,790 --> 00:42:31,390
He's got to be killed
before he starts runnin'.

561
00:42:31,460 --> 00:42:33,990
Look where he is. I can't shoot
that well, and you know it.

562
00:42:34,070 --> 00:42:36,090
[ Atticus ]
I haven't shot a gun in years.

563
00:42:36,170 --> 00:42:39,160
I'd feel mighty comfortable
if you did now.

564
00:42:39,240 --> 00:42:44,870
[ Dog Snarling, Barking ]

565
00:42:44,940 --> 00:42:48,040
[ Snarling, Barking Continue ]

566
00:42:48,110 --> 00:42:51,450
[ Growling ]

567
00:43:03,890 --> 00:43:08,350
[ Growling Continues ]

568
00:43:32,220 --> 00:43:37,320
- Don't go near that dog. He's just
as dangerous dead as alive.
- Yes, sir.

569
00:43:39,060 --> 00:43:42,160
- Atticus?
- Yes, son.

570
00:43:44,900 --> 00:43:48,840
- Nothin'.
- What's the matter, boy?
Can't you talk?

571
00:43:48,910 --> 00:43:52,710
- Didn't you know your daddy is
the best shot in this county?
- Oh, hush, Heck.

572
00:43:52,780 --> 00:43:54,840
Let's get back to town.

573
00:43:54,910 --> 00:43:59,010
- Remember now, don't go near that dog.
- Yes, sir.

574
00:43:59,080 --> 00:44:02,350
I'll send Zeebo out right away
to pick him up.

575
00:44:21,970 --> 00:44:24,700
[ Jem Shouts ]
Hey, Atticus, can we go with ya?

576
00:44:25,980 --> 00:44:28,170
- Can we, please?
- Huh, can we?

577
00:44:28,250 --> 00:44:31,180
I have to go to the country on business,
and you'll just get tired.

578
00:44:31,250 --> 00:44:34,240
Oh, no, not me.
I won't get tired.

579
00:44:34,320 --> 00:44:38,580
Well, you promise to stay in the car
while I talk to Helen Robinson?

580
00:44:38,660 --> 00:44:41,750
- Mm-hmm.
- And not nag me about leaving
if you get tired?

581
00:44:41,830 --> 00:44:43,920
- Huh-uh.
- All right, climb in.

582
00:44:52,140 --> 00:44:56,800
- Who's Helen Robinson?
- She's the wife of the man
I'm defending.

583
00:45:01,310 --> 00:45:03,250
[ Brakes Squeak ]

584
00:45:16,130 --> 00:45:18,930
- Good evening, David.
- Good evening.

585
00:45:19,000 --> 00:45:21,590
- Good evening, Helen.
- Evening, Mr. Finch.

586
00:45:21,670 --> 00:45:24,830
- I came over to tell you
about my visit with Tom.
- Yes, sir.

587
00:45:24,900 --> 00:45:27,230
And to let you know
that I got a postponement of the trial.

588
00:45:27,300 --> 00:45:30,710
[ Atticus Continues, lndistinct ]

589
00:45:47,730 --> 00:45:51,790
[ Leaves Rustling ]

590
00:46:01,570 --> 00:46:04,670
Would you tell my daddy
to come out here, please?

591
00:46:27,900 --> 00:46:30,420
[ Footsteps Approaching ]

592
00:46:54,260 --> 00:46:57,230
You nigger lover.

593
00:47:14,650 --> 00:47:18,240
No need to be afraid of him, son.
He's all bluff.

594
00:47:18,320 --> 00:47:21,680
[ Starts Engine ]

595
00:47:32,960 --> 00:47:34,930
[ Ewell ]
Nigger lover!

596
00:47:46,710 --> 00:47:51,670
There's a lot of ugly things
in this world, son.

597
00:47:51,750 --> 00:47:54,680
I wish I could
keep 'em all away from you.

598
00:47:57,120 --> 00:48:00,650
That's never possible.

599
00:48:00,720 --> 00:48:04,720
Cal, if you wait 'til I get Scout
in bed, I'll drive you home.

600
00:48:04,800 --> 00:48:06,730
Yes, sir.

601
00:48:20,380 --> 00:48:24,580
Jem, do you mind stayin' here with Scout
'til I get Cal home?

602
00:48:24,650 --> 00:48:27,280
- No, sir.
- Night, Jem.

603
00:48:27,350 --> 00:48:30,790
Night, Cal.

604
00:48:30,850 --> 00:48:32,790
[ Car Door Shuts ]

605
00:48:37,730 --> 00:48:40,960
[ Engine Starts, Car Departs ]

606
00:48:41,030 --> 00:48:42,970
[ Rustling ]

607
00:48:54,510 --> 00:48:58,570
[ Rustling Continues ]

608
00:48:58,650 --> 00:49:02,920
[ Rustling ]

609
00:49:02,990 --> 00:49:08,250
[ Rustling ]

610
00:49:08,330 --> 00:49:11,730
[ Rustling Continues ]

611
00:49:11,800 --> 00:49:16,100
[ Bird Screeching ]

612
00:49:16,170 --> 00:49:20,160
Atticus! Atticus!

613
00:49:20,240 --> 00:49:22,170
Atticus!

614
00:49:28,480 --> 00:49:30,540
[ Sighs ]

615
00:49:30,610 --> 00:49:33,550
[ Bird Screeches ]

616
00:49:35,550 --> 00:49:39,720
[ Banging, Creaking ]

617
00:49:39,790 --> 00:49:42,730
[ Banging, Creaking Continue ]

618
00:49:49,930 --> 00:49:54,630
[ Footsteps ]

619
00:49:54,710 --> 00:49:57,140
[ Banging, Creaking ]

620
00:49:57,210 --> 00:50:01,140
[ Continues Banging, Creaking ]

621
00:50:09,120 --> 00:50:11,380
[ Bird Screeches ]

622
00:50:33,980 --> 00:50:36,450
[ Banging ]

623
00:50:36,510 --> 00:50:39,210
[ Banging, Creaking ]

624
00:50:39,280 --> 00:50:41,750
[ Banging Continues ]

625
00:50:46,260 --> 00:50:48,990
[ Banging Continues ]

626
00:50:49,060 --> 00:50:51,620
[ Bird Screeches ]

627
00:51:00,140 --> 00:51:03,160
[ Adult Scout ]
Atticus had promised me
he would wear me out...

628
00:51:03,240 --> 00:51:05,900
if he ever heard
of me fightin' anymore.

629
00:51:05,980 --> 00:51:09,570
I was far too old and too big
for such childish things.

630
00:51:09,650 --> 00:51:14,980
And the sooner I learned to hold in,
the better off everybody would be.

631
00:51:15,050 --> 00:51:17,990
I soon forgot.

632
00:51:18,060 --> 00:51:21,460
Cecil Jacobs made me forget.

633
00:51:24,330 --> 00:51:27,260
Oh, what is it, Scout?

634
00:51:30,830 --> 00:51:33,060
Atticus, do you defend niggers?

635
00:51:36,470 --> 00:51:39,310
- Don't say "nigger," Scout.
- I didn't say it.

636
00:51:39,380 --> 00:51:42,740
Cecil Jacobs did.
That's why I had to fight him.

637
00:51:42,810 --> 00:51:45,750
Scout,
I don't want you fightin'.

638
00:51:45,820 --> 00:51:48,180
I had to, Atticus.

639
00:51:48,250 --> 00:51:51,240
- He--
- I don't care what the reasons are.

640
00:51:51,320 --> 00:51:55,590
- I forbid you to fight.
- Yes.

641
00:52:01,000 --> 00:52:06,530
Anyway... I'm simply defending
a Negro, Tom Robinson.

642
00:52:07,940 --> 00:52:10,700
Now, Scout,
there are some things that...

643
00:52:10,770 --> 00:52:14,710
you're not old enough
to understand just yet.

644
00:52:16,450 --> 00:52:21,380
There's been some high talk
around town...

645
00:52:21,450 --> 00:52:25,550
to the effect that I shouldn't do much
about defending this man.

646
00:52:27,790 --> 00:52:32,490
If you shouldn't be defending him,
then why are you doing it?

647
00:52:37,630 --> 00:52:41,330
For a number of reasons.

648
00:52:41,410 --> 00:52:44,100
The main one is that,
if I didn't,

649
00:52:44,170 --> 00:52:48,010
I couldn't hold
my head up in town.

650
00:52:48,080 --> 00:52:51,070
I couldn't even tell you or Jem...

651
00:52:51,150 --> 00:52:54,080
not to do something again.

652
00:52:56,850 --> 00:52:58,790
[ Softly ]
Scout.

653
00:53:01,530 --> 00:53:05,590
You're gonna hear some
ugly talk about this in school.

654
00:53:05,660 --> 00:53:10,120
But I want you
to promise me one thing:

655
00:53:10,200 --> 00:53:13,170
That you won't get
into fights over it,

656
00:53:13,240 --> 00:53:16,370
no matter what
they say to you.

657
00:53:20,680 --> 00:53:23,980
- [ Car Approaching ]
- Yes, sir.

658
00:53:33,620 --> 00:53:36,060
[ Car Engine ldling ]

659
00:53:38,260 --> 00:53:41,930
- What are you doing?
- I'm walking like an Egyptian.

660
00:53:42,000 --> 00:53:44,870
- We were studyin' about 'em in school.
- [ Engine Stops ]

661
00:53:44,940 --> 00:53:47,130
Teacher says we wouldn't
be no place without 'em.

662
00:53:47,200 --> 00:53:49,300
Is that so?

663
00:53:49,370 --> 00:53:53,670
Cradle of civilization. They invented
embalming and toilet paper.

664
00:53:56,280 --> 00:53:58,540
That's wrong, Scout.

665
00:54:01,490 --> 00:54:06,550
- You do your feet this way.
- Look, Jem.

666
00:54:21,910 --> 00:54:26,740
Look, the boy wears hair
in front of his eyebrows like you do.

667
00:54:26,810 --> 00:54:30,140
Yeah, and the girl
wears bangs like you.

668
00:54:30,210 --> 00:54:32,650
[ Whispers ]
These are of us.

669
00:55:05,050 --> 00:55:08,110
[ Children Running ]

670
00:55:27,600 --> 00:55:29,540
[ Softly ]
Jem, you awake?

671
00:55:29,610 --> 00:55:33,170
- Go back to bed.
- I can't go to sleep.

672
00:55:33,240 --> 00:55:35,710
Go back to bed.

673
00:55:35,780 --> 00:55:37,710
[ Scout ]
What you got in the box?

674
00:55:37,780 --> 00:55:39,810
Nothin'. Go back to bed.

675
00:55:48,160 --> 00:55:50,090
Come on.

676
00:55:57,400 --> 00:56:00,430
If I show you, will you swear
never to tell anybody?

677
00:56:00,500 --> 00:56:02,940
I swear.

678
00:56:03,010 --> 00:56:05,000
Cross your heart.

679
00:56:16,620 --> 00:56:22,220
[ Watch Ticking ]

680
00:56:22,290 --> 00:56:26,700
[ Ticking Continues ]

681
00:56:26,760 --> 00:56:29,320
- [ Ticking ]
- [ Jem ] I found all of these...

682
00:56:29,400 --> 00:56:32,490
in the knothole of that old tree
at different times.

683
00:56:34,840 --> 00:56:36,770
[ Softly ]
This is a spelling medal.

684
00:56:39,910 --> 00:56:42,710
You know they used to award these
in school to spelling winners...

685
00:56:42,780 --> 00:56:45,150
before we were born.

686
00:56:46,350 --> 00:56:50,050
And another time,
I found this.

687
00:56:50,120 --> 00:56:54,890
[ Ticking ]

688
00:56:54,960 --> 00:56:58,690
[ Ticking Continues ]

689
00:57:04,070 --> 00:57:06,000
And this.

690
00:57:19,230 --> 00:57:22,760
Scout...

691
00:57:22,830 --> 00:57:26,670
you know somethin' else I never told you
about that night I went back...

692
00:57:26,740 --> 00:57:28,760
- to the Radley house?
- "Somethin' else"?

693
00:57:28,840 --> 00:57:31,430
You never told me anything
about that night.

694
00:57:31,510 --> 00:57:34,540
Well...

695
00:57:34,610 --> 00:57:37,450
the first time when I was
gettin' out of my britches?

696
00:57:37,520 --> 00:57:41,110
- Uh-huh.
- Well, they was all in a tangle,

697
00:57:41,190 --> 00:57:44,590
and I couldn't get 'em loose.

698
00:57:44,660 --> 00:57:48,790
Well, when I went back, though,

699
00:57:48,860 --> 00:57:52,300
they were folded
across the fence,

700
00:57:52,360 --> 00:57:54,700
sort of like
they was expectin' me.

701
00:57:59,700 --> 00:58:02,170
[ Watch Ticking ]

702
00:58:02,240 --> 00:58:05,070
[ Adult Scout ]
It was to be a long time...

703
00:58:05,140 --> 00:58:08,480
before Jem and I talked
about Boo again.

704
00:58:10,880 --> 00:58:13,940
School finally ended
and summer came...

705
00:58:14,020 --> 00:58:16,390
and so did Dill.

706
00:58:16,460 --> 00:58:19,910
- Good mornin'.
- Good mornin'. My, you're up...

707
00:58:19,990 --> 00:58:21,580
mighty bright and early.

708
00:58:21,660 --> 00:58:23,420
Ooh, I been up since 4:00.

709
00:58:23,500 --> 00:58:27,430
- [ Cal ] 4:00?
- Oh, yes, I always get up at 4:00.

710
00:58:27,500 --> 00:58:29,430
It's in my blood.

711
00:58:29,500 --> 00:58:32,940
My daddy was a railroad man
'til he got rich.

712
00:58:33,000 --> 00:58:34,560
Now he flies airplanes.

713
00:58:34,640 --> 00:58:36,130
One of these days,

714
00:58:36,210 --> 00:58:38,300
he's just gonna swoop
down here at Maycomb,

715
00:58:38,380 --> 00:58:40,640
pick me up and take me
for a ride.

716
00:58:49,250 --> 00:58:52,310
Who's that in the car
with Sheriff Tate?

717
00:58:52,390 --> 00:58:55,090
Tom Robinson, son.

718
00:58:55,160 --> 00:58:57,090
Where's he been?

719
00:58:57,160 --> 00:59:00,460
In the Abbottsville jail.

720
00:59:00,530 --> 00:59:04,470
- Why?
- The sheriff thought
he'd be safer there.

721
00:59:04,540 --> 00:59:07,130
They're bringin' him
back here tonight...

722
00:59:07,210 --> 00:59:09,500
because his trial
is tomorrow.

723
00:59:18,850 --> 00:59:22,310
[ Crickets Chirping ]

724
00:59:22,390 --> 00:59:24,480
[ Knocking ]

725
00:59:26,990 --> 00:59:30,360
- Well, good evenin', Heck.
- Good evenin', Mr. Finch.

726
00:59:31,500 --> 00:59:33,430
Come in.

727
00:59:37,500 --> 00:59:42,500
The news has gotten around
the county about my bringin'
Tom Robinson back to the jail.

728
00:59:42,570 --> 00:59:47,410
I, uh, heard there might be trouble
from that bunch out at Old Sarum.

729
00:59:55,850 --> 01:00:00,450
Cal, if I need you to stay here tonight,
can you do it?

730
01:00:00,530 --> 01:00:03,020
- Yes, sir, I can.
- Thank you.

731
01:00:05,660 --> 01:00:08,100
[ Dishes Clattering ]

732
01:00:09,800 --> 01:00:11,740
I think you better
count on stayin'.

733
01:00:11,800 --> 01:00:14,430
Yes, sir.

734
01:00:34,530 --> 01:00:38,720
[ Door Opens, Shuts ]

735
01:00:40,430 --> 01:00:44,270
[ Car Door Opens, Shuts ]

736
01:00:44,340 --> 01:00:48,600
[ Engine Starts,
Car Departs ]

737
01:00:48,670 --> 01:00:51,610
- What's goin' on?
- Shh. Go back to sleep.

738
01:00:51,680 --> 01:00:55,110
- [ Scout ] What's goin' on?
- Shh.

739
01:01:03,760 --> 01:01:06,190
[ Jem Whispers ]
Hey, there's his car.

740
01:01:18,100 --> 01:01:21,470
[ Whispering ]
There he is over there.

741
01:01:24,080 --> 01:01:28,170
No, Scout, don't go to him.
He might not like it.

742
01:01:28,250 --> 01:01:33,780
I just wanted to see where he was
and what he was up to.

743
01:01:33,850 --> 01:01:36,290
He's all right.
Let's go back home.

744
01:01:36,350 --> 01:01:38,290
Come on.

745
01:01:39,420 --> 01:01:41,360
[ Cars Approaching ]

746
01:01:45,330 --> 01:01:47,760
[ Cars Approaching ]

747
01:02:10,590 --> 01:02:14,080
[ Brakes Squeaking ]

748
01:02:15,990 --> 01:02:17,930
[ Engines Stop ]

749
01:02:35,950 --> 01:02:40,180
- He in there, Mr. Finch?
- He is.

750
01:02:40,250 --> 01:02:43,740
He's asleep.
Don't wake him.

751
01:02:43,820 --> 01:02:46,590
You know what we want.

752
01:02:48,530 --> 01:02:51,650
Get aside from that door,
Mr. Finch.

753
01:02:53,830 --> 01:02:55,770
Walter...

754
01:02:57,770 --> 01:03:01,210
I think you oughta turn
right around and go back home.

755
01:03:01,270 --> 01:03:05,070
- Heck Tate's around here somewhere.
- [ Man ] No, he ain't!

756
01:03:05,140 --> 01:03:08,340
Heck and his bunch is out in Old Sarum,
lookin' for us.

757
01:03:08,410 --> 01:03:10,810
[ Man #2 ] We knowed he was,
so we come in this other way.

758
01:03:10,880 --> 01:03:14,780
- You ain't thought
about that, had you, Mr. Finch?
- [ Atticus ] I thought about it.

759
01:03:14,850 --> 01:03:19,790
- I can't see Atticus.
- [ Atticus ]
Well, that changes things some.

760
01:03:19,860 --> 01:03:23,290
[ Scout ]
Atticus.

761
01:03:25,000 --> 01:03:28,120
- Hey, Atticus.
- Jem, go home.

762
01:03:28,200 --> 01:03:32,360
Take Scout and Dill
home with you.

763
01:03:32,440 --> 01:03:35,870
- Son, I said go home.
- No, sir.

764
01:03:38,040 --> 01:03:40,070
- I'll send him home.
- Don't you touch him!

765
01:03:40,150 --> 01:03:42,200
Let him go!
Let him go!

766
01:03:42,280 --> 01:03:44,210
That'll do, Scout.

767
01:03:47,090 --> 01:03:50,150
Ain't nobody gonna
do Jem that way!

768
01:03:51,320 --> 01:03:53,290
You get 'em out of here,
Mr. Finch.

769
01:03:57,930 --> 01:04:01,090
Jem, I want you to please leave.

770
01:04:01,170 --> 01:04:03,500
- No, sir.
- Jem!

771
01:04:03,570 --> 01:04:06,870
I tell you, I ain't goin'.

772
01:04:06,940 --> 01:04:08,870
Hey, Mr. Cunningham.

773
01:04:11,540 --> 01:04:16,850
I said, "Hey, Mr. Cunningham."
How's your entailment gettin' along?

774
01:04:18,150 --> 01:04:22,680
Don't you remember me,
Mr. Cunningham?

775
01:04:22,750 --> 01:04:27,490
I'm Jean Louise Finch.

776
01:04:27,560 --> 01:04:31,760
You brought us some hickory nuts
one morning. Remember?

777
01:04:31,830 --> 01:04:35,030
We had a talk.

778
01:04:35,100 --> 01:04:39,260
I went and got my daddy
to come out and thank you.

779
01:04:39,340 --> 01:04:43,640
I go to school with your boy.
I go to school with Walter.

780
01:04:43,710 --> 01:04:45,940
He's a nice boy.

781
01:04:46,010 --> 01:04:48,500
Tell him "hey" for me,
won't you?

782
01:04:48,580 --> 01:04:51,670
You know somethin',
Mr. Cunningham?

783
01:04:51,750 --> 01:04:53,740
Entailments are bad.

784
01:04:53,820 --> 01:04:57,080
- Entailments--
- [ Men Shuffling ]

785
01:04:58,720 --> 01:05:01,390
[ Coughing ]

786
01:05:03,290 --> 01:05:08,290
Atticus, I was just sayin'
to Mr. Cunningham
that entailments were bad,

787
01:05:08,370 --> 01:05:10,560
but not to worry.

788
01:05:10,640 --> 01:05:13,230
It takes a long time sometimes.

789
01:05:14,940 --> 01:05:16,870
What's the matter?

790
01:05:25,080 --> 01:05:28,750
I sure meant no harm,
Mr. Cunningham.

791
01:05:32,520 --> 01:05:35,650
No harm taken, young lady.

792
01:05:38,330 --> 01:05:40,260
I'll tell Walter
you said, "Hey."

793
01:05:41,470 --> 01:05:43,590
Let's clear outta here.

794
01:05:45,100 --> 01:05:47,870
Let's go, boys.

795
01:05:47,940 --> 01:05:52,210
[ Men Murmuring ]

796
01:05:58,080 --> 01:06:00,380
[ Engines Starting ]

797
01:06:00,450 --> 01:06:04,390
[ Engines Revvng ]

798
01:06:10,830 --> 01:06:15,560
- [ Cars Departing ]
- Now you can go home.

799
01:06:15,630 --> 01:06:18,120
All of you.

800
01:06:18,200 --> 01:06:20,470
I'll be there later.

801
01:06:22,110 --> 01:06:24,510
Come on.

802
01:06:24,580 --> 01:06:26,510
Come on.

803
01:06:51,000 --> 01:06:55,910
[ Tom Whispers ]
Mr. Finch, are they gone?

804
01:06:55,970 --> 01:07:00,340
They've gone.
They won't bother you anymore.

805
01:07:20,670 --> 01:07:22,600
[ Jem ]
'Morning, Mr. Strikes.

806
01:07:22,670 --> 01:07:25,430
- How do you do?
- Ever seen so many people?

807
01:07:25,500 --> 01:07:27,970
Just like on Saturday.

808
01:07:29,810 --> 01:07:31,740
Where you goin'?

809
01:07:31,810 --> 01:07:35,340
I can't stand it any longer.
I'm goin' to the courthouse and watch.

810
01:07:35,410 --> 01:07:39,110
- You better not.
You know what Atticus said.
- I don't care if he did.

811
01:07:39,180 --> 01:07:42,950
I'm not gonna miss
the most exciting thing that
ever happened in this town.

812
01:07:59,970 --> 01:08:02,410
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

813
01:08:02,470 --> 01:08:06,410
It's packed solid.
They're standin' all along the back.

814
01:08:07,910 --> 01:08:10,470
- Reverend.
- Yes?

815
01:08:10,550 --> 01:08:14,680
- Reverend Sykes,
are you going upstairs?
- Yes, I am.

816
01:08:14,750 --> 01:08:17,690
[ Crowd Chattering ]

817
01:08:21,830 --> 01:08:24,390
[ Chattering Continues ]

818
01:08:24,460 --> 01:08:27,800
- Thank you, Brother John,
for holding my seat.
- That's okay, Reverend.

819
01:08:27,870 --> 01:08:31,100
Come on, children.
Come on, come on.

820
01:08:31,170 --> 01:08:35,110
[ Baby Crying ]

821
01:08:35,170 --> 01:08:38,230
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

822
01:08:38,310 --> 01:08:42,250
[ Murmuring Continues ]

823
01:08:56,490 --> 01:09:00,400
[ Murmuring Continues ]

824
01:09:04,770 --> 01:09:07,760
[ Clerk ]
This court's now in session.
Everybody rise.

825
01:09:12,140 --> 01:09:16,310
- [ Gavel Pounding ]
- [ Crowd Quiets ]

826
01:09:19,250 --> 01:09:19,620
[ Sheriff ]
On the night of August 21,

827
01:09:19,620 --> 01:09:22,250
[ Sheriff ]
On the night of August 21,

828
01:09:22,320 --> 01:09:26,190
I was just leaving my office
to go home when Bob--

829
01:09:26,260 --> 01:09:28,660
Mr. Ewell came in.

830
01:09:28,730 --> 01:09:30,820
Very excited he was,

831
01:09:30,900 --> 01:09:34,630
and he said to get to his house
as quick as I could,

832
01:09:34,700 --> 01:09:37,070
that his girl
had been raped.

833
01:09:37,140 --> 01:09:41,700
I got in my car and went out
there as fast as I could.

834
01:09:41,770 --> 01:09:44,740
She was pretty well beat up.

835
01:09:44,810 --> 01:09:48,610
I asked her if Tom Robinson
beat her like that.

836
01:09:48,680 --> 01:09:52,170
She said, yes, he had.

837
01:09:52,250 --> 01:09:55,580
I asked if he'd
taken advantage of her.

838
01:09:55,650 --> 01:09:58,680
She said, "Yes, he did."

839
01:09:58,760 --> 01:10:02,820
That's all there was to it.

840
01:10:02,890 --> 01:10:06,630
[ Prosecutor ]
Thank you.

841
01:10:06,700 --> 01:10:10,930
[ Atticus ]
Did anybody call a doctor, Sheriff?

842
01:10:11,000 --> 01:10:13,770
- No, sir.
- Why not?

843
01:10:13,840 --> 01:10:16,100
Well, I didn't think
it was necessary.

844
01:10:16,170 --> 01:10:18,510
She was pretty well beat up.

845
01:10:18,580 --> 01:10:20,700
Somethin' sure happened.
It was obvious.

846
01:10:23,050 --> 01:10:28,180
Now, Sheriff, you say that she was
mighty beat up. In what way?

847
01:10:28,250 --> 01:10:31,120
Well, she was beaten
around the head.

848
01:10:31,190 --> 01:10:34,280
There were bruises
already comin' on her arms.

849
01:10:34,360 --> 01:10:37,490
She had a black eye startin'.

850
01:10:37,560 --> 01:10:40,430
- Which eye?
- [ Sheriff ] Let's see.

851
01:10:40,500 --> 01:10:43,090
It was her left.

852
01:10:43,170 --> 01:10:47,160
Well, now, was that, uh--

853
01:10:47,240 --> 01:10:49,970
That was her left,
facing you...

854
01:10:50,040 --> 01:10:52,030
or looking the way
that you were?

855
01:10:52,110 --> 01:10:57,450
Oh, yes, that would make it
her right eye.

856
01:10:57,520 --> 01:11:00,680
It was her right eye, Mr. Finch.
Now I remember.

857
01:11:00,750 --> 01:11:05,120
She was beaten up
on that side of her face.

858
01:11:06,690 --> 01:11:08,720
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

859
01:11:10,060 --> 01:11:12,550
Which side again, Heck?

860
01:11:12,630 --> 01:11:15,460
The right side.

861
01:11:15,530 --> 01:11:19,160
She had bruises
on her arms and...

862
01:11:19,240 --> 01:11:21,170
she showed me her neck.

863
01:11:21,240 --> 01:11:25,700
There were definite finger marks
on her gullet.

864
01:11:25,780 --> 01:11:27,710
Well...

865
01:11:27,780 --> 01:11:32,880
all around her neck,
at the back of her throat?

866
01:11:34,820 --> 01:11:36,840
I'd say they were all around.

867
01:11:45,260 --> 01:11:47,200
[ Judge ]
The witness may be seated.

868
01:11:48,800 --> 01:11:50,730
[ Clerk ]
Robert E. Lee Ewell.

869
01:11:50,800 --> 01:11:53,930
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

870
01:11:55,510 --> 01:11:57,500
Place your hand
on the Bible, please.

871
01:11:57,580 --> 01:12:01,380
Do you solemnly swear to tell the truth,
the whole truth, and nothing but
the truth, so help you God?

872
01:12:01,450 --> 01:12:03,710
- I do.
- Sit down, please.

873
01:12:06,950 --> 01:12:08,890
Now, Mr. Ewell,

874
01:12:08,950 --> 01:12:11,980
you tell us,
just in your own words,

875
01:12:12,060 --> 01:12:14,820
what happened on August 21.

876
01:12:14,890 --> 01:12:16,830
[ Ewell ]
Well, that night...

877
01:12:16,890 --> 01:12:19,760
I was comin' from the woods
with a load of kindlin'...

878
01:12:19,830 --> 01:12:23,130
and I heard Mayella screamin'
as I got to the fence.

879
01:12:23,200 --> 01:12:27,800
So I dropped my kindlin',
and I run as fast as I could,
but I run into the fence.

880
01:12:27,870 --> 01:12:32,330
But when I got loose,
I run up to the window,

881
01:12:32,410 --> 01:12:35,000
and I seen him
with my Mayella!

882
01:12:35,080 --> 01:12:38,340
- [ Crowd Murmuring ]
- [ Gavel Pounding ]

883
01:12:38,420 --> 01:12:40,710
[ Crowd Quiets ]

884
01:12:40,790 --> 01:12:44,450
What did you do
after you saw the defendant?

885
01:12:44,520 --> 01:12:46,460
I run around the house
tryin' to get in,

886
01:12:46,520 --> 01:12:49,760
but he done run through the front door
just ahead of me!

887
01:12:49,830 --> 01:12:51,820
But I seen who it was, all right!

888
01:12:51,900 --> 01:12:57,300
- I seen him.
- [ Crowd Murmuring ]

889
01:12:57,370 --> 01:13:01,070
And I run in the house and...

890
01:13:01,140 --> 01:13:05,370
poor Mayella was layin'
on the floor squallin'.

891
01:13:05,440 --> 01:13:08,840
Then I run for Mr. Tate
just as quick as I could.

892
01:13:08,910 --> 01:13:12,540
Mm-hmm.
Thank you, Mr. Ewell.

893
01:13:17,620 --> 01:13:22,120
- [ Crowd Laughing ]
- [ Laughing ]

894
01:13:22,190 --> 01:13:26,690
Would you mind if I just,
uh, ask you a few questions, Mr. Ewell?

895
01:13:26,760 --> 01:13:29,200
No, sir, Mr. Finch,
I sure wouldn't.

896
01:13:34,640 --> 01:13:37,230
Folks were doin' a lot
of running that night.

897
01:13:37,310 --> 01:13:42,080
You say that you ran to the house,
you ran to the window,

898
01:13:42,150 --> 01:13:46,640
you ran inside, you ran to Mayella,
you ran to Sheriff Tate.

899
01:13:46,720 --> 01:13:51,090
Did you, during all this running,
run for a doctor?

900
01:13:51,160 --> 01:13:53,990
There wasn't no need to.
I seen who done it.

901
01:13:56,530 --> 01:13:59,930
Now, Mr. Ewell, you've heard
the sheriff's testimony.

902
01:14:00,000 --> 01:14:02,260
Do you agree with his description
of Mayella's injuries?

903
01:14:02,330 --> 01:14:06,700
I agree with everything
Mr. Tate said.

904
01:14:06,770 --> 01:14:08,710
Her eye was blacked.

905
01:14:08,770 --> 01:14:12,400
She was mighty beat up,
mighty beat up.

906
01:14:14,680 --> 01:14:19,980
Now, Mr. Ewell, can you, uh...
can you read and write?

907
01:14:22,290 --> 01:14:26,150
Yes, sir, Mr. Finch,
I can read and I can write.

908
01:14:28,060 --> 01:14:30,320
Good.

909
01:14:30,390 --> 01:14:33,630
- Then will you write your name, please.
- [ Crowd Murmuring ]

910
01:14:33,700 --> 01:14:36,890
Write there.
Show us.

911
01:15:08,230 --> 01:15:10,170
So what's so interesting?

912
01:15:10,230 --> 01:15:12,170
You're left-handed, Mr. Ewell.

913
01:15:12,240 --> 01:15:16,430
What's that got to do with it, Judge?
I'm a God-fearin' man.

914
01:15:16,510 --> 01:15:19,140
That Atticus Finch,
he's tryin' to take advantage of me!

915
01:15:19,210 --> 01:15:21,440
You got to watch tricky lawyers
like Atticus Finch!

916
01:15:21,510 --> 01:15:24,280
Quiet. Quiet, sir.

917
01:15:24,350 --> 01:15:27,280
Now the witness
may take his seat.

918
01:15:27,350 --> 01:15:32,550
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

919
01:15:37,390 --> 01:15:39,490
[ Clerk ]
Mayella Violet Ewell.

920
01:15:51,640 --> 01:15:55,240
Put your hand
on the Bible, please.

921
01:15:55,310 --> 01:15:57,250
Do you solemnly swear
to tell the truth,

922
01:15:57,310 --> 01:16:00,340
the whole truth and nothing
but the truth, so help you God?

923
01:16:00,420 --> 01:16:02,410
Sit down, please.

924
01:16:06,320 --> 01:16:08,790
[ Prosecutor ]
Now, Mayella...

925
01:16:10,160 --> 01:16:14,860
suppose you tell us
just what happened, huh?

926
01:16:14,930 --> 01:16:19,430
Well, sir--
[ Clears Throat ]

927
01:16:19,500 --> 01:16:23,940
Sir... I was s-sittin'
on the porch...

928
01:16:24,010 --> 01:16:26,940
and he come along.

929
01:16:29,010 --> 01:16:33,070
There's this old chifforobe
in the yard,

930
01:16:33,150 --> 01:16:36,610
and l-I said,

931
01:16:36,690 --> 01:16:39,350
"You come in here, boy,
and bust up this chifforobe,

932
01:16:39,420 --> 01:16:42,550
and I'll give you a nickel."

933
01:16:42,630 --> 01:16:45,220
So he come on in the yard,

934
01:16:45,300 --> 01:16:49,390
and I go in the house
to get him the nickel...

935
01:16:49,470 --> 01:16:52,900
and I turn around
and before I know it, he's on me.

936
01:16:54,640 --> 01:16:56,610
I fought and hollered...

937
01:16:56,670 --> 01:16:59,270
but he had me
around the neck...

938
01:16:59,340 --> 01:17:03,400
and he hit me,
again and again.

939
01:17:04,920 --> 01:17:10,110
And the next thing I knew,
Papa was in the room...

940
01:17:10,190 --> 01:17:15,890
a-standin' over me, hollerin',
"Who done it? Who done it?"

941
01:17:15,960 --> 01:17:18,430
Thank you, Mayella.

942
01:17:20,000 --> 01:17:22,590
Your witness, Atticus.

943
01:17:25,840 --> 01:17:31,670
Miss Mayella,
is your father good to you?

944
01:17:31,740 --> 01:17:35,800
I mean, is he easy
to get along with?

945
01:17:37,110 --> 01:17:39,140
Does tolerable.

946
01:17:39,220 --> 01:17:41,550
Except when he's drinking.

947
01:17:43,850 --> 01:17:47,650
When he's riled...
has he ever beaten you?

948
01:17:53,660 --> 01:17:58,030
My pa's never touched
a hair on my head in my life.

949
01:18:02,470 --> 01:18:08,000
You say that you asked Tom to come in
and chop up a-- What was it?

950
01:18:09,110 --> 01:18:11,080
A chifforobe.

951
01:18:11,150 --> 01:18:16,420
Was that the first time that you ever
asked him to come inside the fence?

952
01:18:16,490 --> 01:18:19,680
Yes.

953
01:18:19,760 --> 01:18:25,090
Didn't you ever ask him to come
inside the fence before?

954
01:18:29,000 --> 01:18:31,430
I might have.

955
01:18:31,500 --> 01:18:34,440
[ Atticus ]
Can you remember any other occasion?

956
01:18:34,510 --> 01:18:37,130
No.

957
01:18:37,210 --> 01:18:42,870
You say, "He caught me, he choked me,
and he took advantage of me."

958
01:18:42,950 --> 01:18:44,880
Is that right?

959
01:18:48,990 --> 01:18:51,320
Do you remember him
beating you about the face?

960
01:18:53,160 --> 01:18:55,250
No.

961
01:18:55,330 --> 01:18:58,760
I don't...
[ Clears Throat ]

962
01:18:58,830 --> 01:19:01,590
recollect if he hit me.

963
01:19:05,270 --> 01:19:09,210
I mean, y-yes!
H-He hit me!

964
01:19:09,270 --> 01:19:11,940
- He hit me!
- [ Crowd Murmuring ]

965
01:19:12,010 --> 01:19:16,280
[ Murmuring Continues ]

966
01:19:16,350 --> 01:19:18,280
Thank you.

967
01:19:18,350 --> 01:19:21,110
Now will you identify
the man who beat you.

968
01:19:21,190 --> 01:19:24,450
I most certainly will.
Sittin' right yonder.

969
01:19:26,020 --> 01:19:29,460
Tom, will you stand up, please?

970
01:19:29,530 --> 01:19:33,360
Let Miss Mayella
have a good long look at you.

971
01:19:41,370 --> 01:19:45,330
Tom, will you
catch this, please?

972
01:19:48,910 --> 01:19:50,850
Thank you.

973
01:19:53,980 --> 01:19:58,010
Now, then, this time, will you please
catch it with your left hand.

974
01:19:58,090 --> 01:20:00,580
I can't, sir.

975
01:20:00,660 --> 01:20:02,590
[ Atticus ]
Why can't you?

976
01:20:02,660 --> 01:20:05,590
I can't use
my left hand at all.

977
01:20:05,660 --> 01:20:09,600
I got it caught in a cotton gin
when I was 12 years old.

978
01:20:09,670 --> 01:20:12,600
All my muscles were tore loose.

979
01:20:12,670 --> 01:20:17,770
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

980
01:20:17,840 --> 01:20:20,110
[ Crowd Quiets ]

981
01:20:29,190 --> 01:20:32,210
Is this the man
who raped you?

982
01:20:34,360 --> 01:20:37,120
It most certainly is.

983
01:20:37,190 --> 01:20:39,130
[ Atticus ]
How?

984
01:20:41,700 --> 01:20:45,690
I don't know how.
He done it.

985
01:20:45,770 --> 01:20:47,900
[ Crying ]
He just done it.

986
01:20:47,970 --> 01:20:50,740
You have testified...

987
01:20:50,810 --> 01:20:55,210
that he choked you
and he beat you.

988
01:20:55,280 --> 01:21:00,510
You didn't say that he sneaked up
behind you and knocked you out cold,

989
01:21:00,580 --> 01:21:04,580
but that you turned around...

990
01:21:04,660 --> 01:21:07,750
and there he was.

991
01:21:07,830 --> 01:21:11,920
Do you want to tell us
what really happened?

992
01:21:12,000 --> 01:21:13,930
[ Gasps ]

993
01:21:14,000 --> 01:21:17,930
I got something to say...

994
01:21:18,000 --> 01:21:21,600
and then I ain't
gonna say no more!

995
01:21:25,010 --> 01:21:28,270
He took advantage of me!

996
01:21:30,250 --> 01:21:35,780
And if you fine,
fancy gentlemen...

997
01:21:35,850 --> 01:21:37,950
ain't gonna do
nothing about it,

998
01:21:38,020 --> 01:21:43,290
then you're just a bunch of lousy,
yellow, stinkin' cowards!

999
01:21:43,360 --> 01:21:45,730
The whole bunch of ya!

1000
01:21:45,800 --> 01:21:49,060
And your fancy airs
don't come to nothin'!

1001
01:21:49,130 --> 01:21:52,070
Your "ma'am"-ing
and your "Miss Mayella"-ing,

1002
01:21:52,140 --> 01:21:55,400
it don't come to nothin',
Mr. Finch!

1003
01:21:55,470 --> 01:21:58,370
N-N--
[ Gasps ]

1004
01:21:58,440 --> 01:22:00,770
- [ Crowd Murmuring ]
- [ Mayella Gasps ]

1005
01:22:00,840 --> 01:22:03,280
You sit down now.

1006
01:22:03,350 --> 01:22:09,080
[ Murmuring Continues ]

1007
01:22:12,660 --> 01:22:14,720
[ Gavel Pounding ]

1008
01:22:14,790 --> 01:22:17,520
[ Crowd Quiets ]

1009
01:22:19,230 --> 01:22:21,750
Atticus.

1010
01:22:24,330 --> 01:22:26,360
Mr. Gilmer.

1011
01:22:28,840 --> 01:22:30,770
[ Clears Throat ]

1012
01:22:30,840 --> 01:22:33,610
The State rests, Judge.

1013
01:22:41,020 --> 01:22:43,610
[ Clerk ]
Tom Robinson, take the stand.

1014
01:22:58,800 --> 01:23:00,930
Put your hand on the Bible.

1015
01:23:01,000 --> 01:23:03,060
Do you solemnly swear
to tell the truth,

1016
01:23:03,140 --> 01:23:05,230
the whole truth and nothing
but the truth, so help you God?

1017
01:23:05,310 --> 01:23:07,400
- I do.
- Sit down.

1018
01:23:19,160 --> 01:23:21,750
Now, Tom...

1019
01:23:21,830 --> 01:23:25,260
were you acquainted
with Mayella Violet Ewell?

1020
01:23:25,330 --> 01:23:31,100
Yes, sir. I had to pass her place
going to and from the field every day.

1021
01:23:31,170 --> 01:23:35,260
- Is there any other way to go?
- No, sir.

1022
01:23:35,340 --> 01:23:37,600
None's I know of.

1023
01:23:37,670 --> 01:23:42,110
- And did she ever speak to you?
- Why, yes, sir.

1024
01:23:42,180 --> 01:23:45,210
I'd tip my hat when I'd go by.

1025
01:23:45,280 --> 01:23:49,380
One day she asked me
to come inside the fence...

1026
01:23:49,450 --> 01:23:52,550
and bust up
a chifforobe for her.

1027
01:23:52,620 --> 01:23:55,250
She give me the hatchet,
and I broke it up.

1028
01:23:55,330 --> 01:24:00,190
And then she said, "l reckon I'll
have to give you a nickel, won't l?"

1029
01:24:00,260 --> 01:24:03,390
And I said, "No, ma'am.
There ain't no charge."

1030
01:24:03,470 --> 01:24:06,900
And I went home.

1031
01:24:06,970 --> 01:24:11,700
Mr. Finch, that was way last spring.
Way over a year ago.

1032
01:24:11,780 --> 01:24:15,180
Did you ever go
on the place again?

1033
01:24:18,320 --> 01:24:20,750
Yes, sir.

1034
01:24:20,820 --> 01:24:22,750
[ Atticus ]
When?

1035
01:24:25,160 --> 01:24:28,590
Well, I went lots of times.

1036
01:24:28,660 --> 01:24:31,590
Seemed like every time
I passed by yonder,

1037
01:24:31,660 --> 01:24:34,100
she would have
some little something for me to do:.

1038
01:24:34,160 --> 01:24:37,000
chopping kindlin'
and totin' water for her.

1039
01:24:45,280 --> 01:24:47,370
Tom...

1040
01:24:47,440 --> 01:24:50,380
what happened to you...

1041
01:24:50,450 --> 01:24:54,710
on the evening of August 21
of last year?

1042
01:25:02,130 --> 01:25:07,890
Mr. Finch, I was going home
as usual that evening.

1043
01:25:09,800 --> 01:25:12,740
When I passed the Ewell place,

1044
01:25:12,800 --> 01:25:16,670
Miss Mayella were on the porch,
like she said she were.

1045
01:25:16,740 --> 01:25:21,070
She said for me to come there
and help her a minute.

1046
01:25:21,140 --> 01:25:23,410
Well, I went inside the fence,

1047
01:25:23,480 --> 01:25:27,250
and I looked around
for some kindlin' to work on,

1048
01:25:27,320 --> 01:25:29,340
but I didn't see none.

1049
01:25:31,320 --> 01:25:36,660
Then she said to come in the house,
she has a door needs fixin'.

1050
01:25:36,730 --> 01:25:40,660
So I follows her inside,
and I looked at the door...

1051
01:25:40,730 --> 01:25:44,430
and it looked all right.

1052
01:25:44,500 --> 01:25:46,530
Then she shut the door.

1053
01:25:48,610 --> 01:25:53,240
All the time I was wondering
why it was so quiet.

1054
01:25:54,780 --> 01:25:57,210
Then it come to me.

1055
01:25:57,280 --> 01:26:00,550
There was not a child
on the place.

1056
01:26:00,620 --> 01:26:05,820
And I said, "Miss Mayella,
where are the children?"

1057
01:26:05,890 --> 01:26:10,550
She said,
"They all gone to get ice cream."

1058
01:26:10,630 --> 01:26:15,690
She said it took her a year to save
seven nickels, but she'd done it.

1059
01:26:15,770 --> 01:26:19,070
And they'd all gone to town.

1060
01:26:19,140 --> 01:26:21,200
What did you say then?

1061
01:26:21,270 --> 01:26:26,730
Well, I said something like...

1062
01:26:26,810 --> 01:26:31,910
"Why, Miss Mayella,
that's right nice of you to treat 'em."

1063
01:26:31,980 --> 01:26:34,580
She said, "You think so?"

1064
01:26:34,650 --> 01:26:39,750
Well, I said, "l best be goin'."
I couldn't do nothin' for her.

1065
01:26:39,820 --> 01:26:42,420
And she said I could.

1066
01:26:42,490 --> 01:26:45,520
I asked her what.

1067
01:26:45,600 --> 01:26:50,030
And she said to just step
on the chair yonder...

1068
01:26:50,100 --> 01:26:55,040
and get that box down
from on top of the chifforobe.

1069
01:26:55,110 --> 01:26:59,040
So I done like she told me,

1070
01:26:59,110 --> 01:27:02,040
and I was reachin'...

1071
01:27:02,110 --> 01:27:07,410
when the next thing I knows,
she grabbed me around the legs.

1072
01:27:07,480 --> 01:27:11,420
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

1073
01:27:14,120 --> 01:27:18,560
She scared me so bad,
I hopped down and turned the chair over.

1074
01:27:18,630 --> 01:27:21,220
That was the only thing--

1075
01:27:21,300 --> 01:27:24,060
only furniture--
disturbed in the room, Mr. Finch,

1076
01:27:24,130 --> 01:27:26,660
I swear, when I left it.

1077
01:27:29,510 --> 01:27:32,480
And what happened after you
turned the chair over?

1078
01:27:39,020 --> 01:27:40,980
Tom?

1079
01:27:46,720 --> 01:27:51,560
You've sworn to tell the whole truth.
Will you do it?

1080
01:27:54,870 --> 01:27:57,930
What happened after that?

1081
01:28:02,010 --> 01:28:05,170
Mr. Finch,

1082
01:28:05,240 --> 01:28:07,970
I got down off the chair,

1083
01:28:08,040 --> 01:28:11,310
and I turned around...

1084
01:28:11,380 --> 01:28:14,180
and she sorta jumped on me.

1085
01:28:15,320 --> 01:28:18,340
She hugged me
around the waist.

1086
01:28:18,420 --> 01:28:22,760
She reached up and kissed me
on the face.

1087
01:28:22,830 --> 01:28:27,630
She said she'd never kissed
a grown man before...

1088
01:28:27,700 --> 01:28:31,330
and she might as well kiss me.

1089
01:28:31,400 --> 01:28:35,500
She says for me
to kiss her back,

1090
01:28:35,570 --> 01:28:39,570
and I said,
"Miss Mayella, let me outta here,"

1091
01:28:39,640 --> 01:28:41,580
and I tried to run.

1092
01:28:43,410 --> 01:28:48,720
Mr. Ewell cussed at her
from the window.

1093
01:28:48,790 --> 01:28:50,880
He said he's gonna kill her.

1094
01:28:59,430 --> 01:29:01,360
[ Atticus ]
And what happened after that?

1095
01:29:05,000 --> 01:29:09,600
I was runnin' so fast,
I don't know what happened.

1096
01:29:17,750 --> 01:29:21,410
Tom, did you rape
Mayella Ewell?

1097
01:29:25,620 --> 01:29:28,820
I did not, sir.

1098
01:29:28,890 --> 01:29:32,450
[ Atticus ]
Did you harm her in any way?

1099
01:29:37,730 --> 01:29:41,860
I did not, sir.

1100
01:30:00,560 --> 01:30:03,920
Robinson,

1101
01:30:03,990 --> 01:30:08,430
you're pretty good at bustin' up
chifforobes and kindlin'
with one hand, aren't ya?

1102
01:30:08,500 --> 01:30:13,530
Strong enough to choke the breath out
of a woman and sling her to the floor?

1103
01:30:13,600 --> 01:30:19,010
- I never done that, sir.
- But you're strong enough to.

1104
01:30:19,080 --> 01:30:23,810
- I reckon so, sir.
- Mm-hmm.

1105
01:30:27,880 --> 01:30:32,550
How come you so all fired anxious
to do that woman's chores?

1106
01:30:35,260 --> 01:30:38,720
Looks like she, she didn't
have nobody to help her.

1107
01:30:38,800 --> 01:30:40,920
Like I said, she--

1108
01:30:41,000 --> 01:30:44,360
With Mr. Ewell and seven children
on the place?

1109
01:30:44,430 --> 01:30:48,460
You did all this choppin' and work
out of sheer goodness, boy?

1110
01:30:48,540 --> 01:30:52,500
[ Chuckles ]
You're a mighty good fella, it seems.

1111
01:30:52,580 --> 01:30:54,980
Did all that
for not one penny?

1112
01:30:56,350 --> 01:31:00,680
Yes, sir.
I felt right sorry for her.

1113
01:31:00,750 --> 01:31:03,510
- She seemed--
- [ Gilmer Chuckling ]

1114
01:31:18,640 --> 01:31:21,830
You felt sorry for her?

1115
01:31:21,910 --> 01:31:25,810
A white woman?

1116
01:31:25,880 --> 01:31:30,010
You felt sorry for her?

1117
01:31:38,990 --> 01:31:42,420
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

1118
01:31:45,230 --> 01:31:48,250
[ Murmuring Continues ]

1119
01:32:01,880 --> 01:32:04,510
To begin with,

1120
01:32:04,580 --> 01:32:07,210
this case should never
have come to trial.

1121
01:32:07,280 --> 01:32:10,380
The State has not produced
one iota...

1122
01:32:10,450 --> 01:32:12,440
of medical evidence...

1123
01:32:12,520 --> 01:32:16,250
that the crime Tom Robinson
is charged with...

1124
01:32:16,330 --> 01:32:19,760
ever took place.

1125
01:32:19,830 --> 01:32:22,160
It has relied, instead,

1126
01:32:22,230 --> 01:32:25,500
upon the testimony
of two witnesses...

1127
01:32:25,570 --> 01:32:28,940
whose evidence has not only been
called into serious question...

1128
01:32:29,010 --> 01:32:31,570
on cross-examination,

1129
01:32:31,640 --> 01:32:36,100
but has been flatly contradicted
by the defendant.

1130
01:32:37,810 --> 01:32:41,250
There is circumstantial evidence
to indicate that...

1131
01:32:41,320 --> 01:32:46,190
Mayella Ewell
was beaten savagely...

1132
01:32:46,260 --> 01:32:50,920
by someone who led,
almost exclusively, with his left.

1133
01:32:53,700 --> 01:32:57,760
Tom Robinson now sits before you
having taken the oath...

1134
01:32:57,830 --> 01:33:01,530
with the only good hand
he possesses,

1135
01:33:01,600 --> 01:33:04,230
his right.

1136
01:33:14,950 --> 01:33:19,680
I have nothing but pity
in my heart...

1137
01:33:19,760 --> 01:33:23,690
for the chief witness
for the State.

1138
01:33:25,860 --> 01:33:31,320
She is the victim
of cruel poverty and ignorance.

1139
01:33:34,970 --> 01:33:38,410
But my pity...

1140
01:33:38,470 --> 01:33:41,640
does not extend so far...

1141
01:33:41,710 --> 01:33:46,050
as to her putting
a man's life at stake,

1142
01:33:46,120 --> 01:33:51,580
which she has done in an effort
to get rid of her own guilt.

1143
01:33:51,650 --> 01:33:54,850
Now I say "guilt," gentlemen,

1144
01:33:54,920 --> 01:34:00,560
because it was guilt
that motivated her.

1145
01:34:01,760 --> 01:34:05,720
She's committed no crime.

1146
01:34:05,800 --> 01:34:08,700
She has merely broken
a rigid and time-honored...

1147
01:34:08,770 --> 01:34:12,470
code of our society--

1148
01:34:12,540 --> 01:34:16,880
a code so severe that whoever breaks it
is hounded from our midst...

1149
01:34:16,950 --> 01:34:19,140
as unfit to live with.

1150
01:34:19,220 --> 01:34:22,410
She must destroy the evidence...

1151
01:34:22,490 --> 01:34:25,180
of her offense.

1152
01:34:25,260 --> 01:34:29,890
But what was the evidence
of her offense?

1153
01:34:29,960 --> 01:34:34,020
Tom Robinson, a human being.

1154
01:34:36,470 --> 01:34:41,300
She must put Tom Robinson
away from her.

1155
01:34:42,840 --> 01:34:46,670
Tom Robinson was to her
a daily reminder...

1156
01:34:46,740 --> 01:34:49,540
of what she did.

1157
01:34:49,610 --> 01:34:52,950
Now, what did she do?

1158
01:34:53,020 --> 01:34:55,140
She tempted a Negro.

1159
01:34:55,220 --> 01:34:59,590
She was white,
and she tempted a Negro.

1160
01:34:59,660 --> 01:35:04,460
She did something that,
in our society, is unspeakable.

1161
01:35:04,530 --> 01:35:07,000
She kissed a black man.

1162
01:35:07,060 --> 01:35:11,330
Not an old uncle,

1163
01:35:11,400 --> 01:35:14,530
but a strong, young Negro man.

1164
01:35:17,570 --> 01:35:21,370
No code mattered to her
before she broke it,

1165
01:35:21,440 --> 01:35:25,640
but it came crashing down
on her afterwards.

1166
01:35:32,590 --> 01:35:36,790
The witnesses for the State,
with the exception of the
sheriff of Maycomb County.

1167
01:35:36,860 --> 01:35:40,230
have presented themselves
to you gentlemen, to this court...

1168
01:35:42,100 --> 01:35:46,190
in the cynical confidence...

1169
01:35:46,270 --> 01:35:50,230
that their testimony
would not be doubted.

1170
01:35:50,310 --> 01:35:55,300
Confident that you gentlemen
would go along with them...

1171
01:35:55,380 --> 01:35:59,040
on the assumption--

1172
01:35:59,120 --> 01:36:01,210
the evil assumption--

1173
01:36:01,280 --> 01:36:04,080
that all Negroes lie,

1174
01:36:04,150 --> 01:36:07,350
all Negroes are basically
immoral beings,

1175
01:36:07,420 --> 01:36:13,060
all Negro men are not
to be trusted around our women.

1176
01:36:15,030 --> 01:36:20,330
An assumption that one associates
with minds of their caliber,

1177
01:36:20,400 --> 01:36:25,170
and which is, in itself,
gentlemen, a lie,

1178
01:36:27,640 --> 01:36:31,700
which I do not need
to point out to you.

1179
01:36:37,150 --> 01:36:40,180
And so,

1180
01:36:40,260 --> 01:36:43,380
a quiet, humble,
respectable Negro,

1181
01:36:43,460 --> 01:36:47,590
who has had the unmitigated
temerity...

1182
01:36:47,660 --> 01:36:52,070
to feel sorry
for a white woman,

1183
01:36:52,140 --> 01:36:58,040
has had to put his word
against two white people's.

1184
01:37:02,480 --> 01:37:05,970
The defendant is not guilty,

1185
01:37:06,050 --> 01:37:10,420
but somebody
in this courtroom is.

1186
01:37:11,350 --> 01:37:14,520
Now, gentlemen,

1187
01:37:14,590 --> 01:37:16,650
in this country,

1188
01:37:16,730 --> 01:37:20,720
our courts are the great levelers.

1189
01:37:20,800 --> 01:37:23,130
In our courts,

1190
01:37:23,200 --> 01:37:28,640
all men are created equal.

1191
01:37:30,210 --> 01:37:34,170
I'm no idealist
to believe firmly...

1192
01:37:34,240 --> 01:37:37,470
in the integrity of our courts
and of our jury system.

1193
01:37:37,550 --> 01:37:42,450
That's no ideal to me.
That is a living, working reality!

1194
01:37:46,460 --> 01:37:51,990
Now I am confident that you
gentlemen will review...

1195
01:37:52,060 --> 01:37:55,260
without passion...

1196
01:37:55,330 --> 01:37:59,560
the evidence
that you have heard,

1197
01:37:59,640 --> 01:38:03,200
come to a decision...

1198
01:38:03,270 --> 01:38:06,470
and restore this man
to his family.

1199
01:38:15,890 --> 01:38:20,790
In the name of God,

1200
01:38:20,860 --> 01:38:24,590
do your duty.

1201
01:38:29,300 --> 01:38:31,560
In the name of God,

1202
01:38:31,630 --> 01:38:34,570
believe...

1203
01:38:34,640 --> 01:38:37,070
Tom Robinson.

1204
01:39:08,470 --> 01:39:11,770
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

1205
01:39:11,840 --> 01:39:15,610
How long has the jury
been out now, Reverend?

1206
01:39:15,680 --> 01:39:17,980
Well, let's see.

1207
01:39:18,050 --> 01:39:21,280
Almost two hours now.

1208
01:39:21,350 --> 01:39:26,410
I think that's
an awful good sign, don't you?

1209
01:39:26,490 --> 01:39:28,460
[ Door Opens ]

1210
01:39:28,520 --> 01:39:32,460
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

1211
01:40:12,870 --> 01:40:15,800
Court's now in session.
Everybody rise.

1212
01:40:35,290 --> 01:40:37,230
Gentlemen of the jury,

1213
01:40:37,290 --> 01:40:41,230
- have you reached a verdict?
- [ Foreman ] We have, Your Honor.

1214
01:40:45,970 --> 01:40:48,660
Will the defendant please rise
and face the jury?

1215
01:40:56,980 --> 01:40:59,040
What is your verdict?

1216
01:40:59,980 --> 01:41:02,010
We find the defendant
guilty as charged.

1217
01:41:02,080 --> 01:41:06,280
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

1218
01:41:22,670 --> 01:41:26,440
Gentlemen, this jury is dismissed.

1219
01:41:26,510 --> 01:41:30,970
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

1220
01:41:31,050 --> 01:41:33,640
[ Clerk ]
Court is adjourned.

1221
01:41:33,720 --> 01:41:35,980
[ Murmuring Continues ]

1222
01:41:36,050 --> 01:41:40,610
[ Crowd Murmuring ]

1223
01:41:44,230 --> 01:41:46,750
I'll go to see Helen
first thing tomorrow morning.

1224
01:41:46,830 --> 01:41:50,420
I told her not to be disappointed,
we'd probably lose this one.

1225
01:41:51,870 --> 01:41:54,300
Tom.

1226
01:42:08,820 --> 01:42:10,840
Yes, Mr. Finch.

1227
01:42:55,460 --> 01:42:58,190
Miss Jean Louise.

1228
01:42:58,270 --> 01:43:01,100
Miss Jean Louise,
stand up.

1229
01:43:05,040 --> 01:43:08,600
Your father's passin'.

1230
01:43:36,070 --> 01:43:38,840
[ Maudie ]
Atticus?

1231
01:43:42,650 --> 01:43:45,710
I-I'm sorry, Atticus.

1232
01:43:45,780 --> 01:43:48,910
- Well, thank you, Maudie.
- [ Car Approaching ]

1233
01:43:56,730 --> 01:44:00,130
Atticus, can I see you
for a minute?

1234
01:44:01,930 --> 01:44:04,560
Will you excuse me?

1235
01:44:28,690 --> 01:44:31,460
Jem?

1236
01:44:31,530 --> 01:44:34,000
Yes'm.

1237
01:44:36,930 --> 01:44:39,990
I don't know if it'll help,

1238
01:44:40,070 --> 01:44:42,540
but I want to say this to you.

1239
01:44:46,710 --> 01:44:50,610
There's some men
in this world...

1240
01:44:50,680 --> 01:44:54,810
who are born to do
our unpleasant jobs for us.

1241
01:44:58,590 --> 01:45:03,150
Your father's one of them.

1242
01:45:05,260 --> 01:45:07,530
- Oh, well.
- [ Car Departing ]

1243
01:45:31,690 --> 01:45:33,620
What's the matter, Atticus?

1244
01:45:38,090 --> 01:45:41,030
Tom Robinson's dead.

1245
01:45:50,440 --> 01:45:54,210
They were taking him
to Abbottsville...

1246
01:45:54,280 --> 01:45:56,840
for safekeeping.

1247
01:45:56,910 --> 01:46:00,870
Tom broke loose and ran.

1248
01:46:00,950 --> 01:46:06,580
The deputy called out
to him to stop,

1249
01:46:06,660 --> 01:46:10,220
and Tom didn't stop.

1250
01:46:10,290 --> 01:46:15,490
He shot at him to wound him
and missed his aim.

1251
01:46:18,400 --> 01:46:20,660
Killed him.

1252
01:46:24,970 --> 01:46:27,140
The deputy says...

1253
01:46:30,510 --> 01:46:35,180
Tom just ran like a crazy man.

1254
01:46:43,390 --> 01:46:48,990
The last thing I told him was not to
lose heart, that we'd ask for an appeal.

1255
01:46:57,310 --> 01:47:00,400
We had such a good chance.

1256
01:47:04,210 --> 01:47:08,010
We had more than a good chance.

1257
01:47:22,200 --> 01:47:25,660
I have to go out
and tell his family.

1258
01:47:25,730 --> 01:47:28,460
You look after
the children, Maudie.

1259
01:47:33,880 --> 01:47:36,710
Atticus, you want me
to go with you?

1260
01:47:36,780 --> 01:47:40,180
No, son, I think I'd better
go out there alone.

1261
01:47:40,250 --> 01:47:43,580
Atticus.

1262
01:47:43,650 --> 01:47:47,460
I'm goin' with you.

1263
01:47:47,540 --> 01:47:49,500
All right, son.

1264
01:47:58,950 --> 01:48:00,880
[ Brakes Squeak ]

1265
01:48:12,830 --> 01:48:17,230
Hello, Mr. Finch.
I'm Spence, Tom's father.

1266
01:48:17,300 --> 01:48:19,890
Hello, Spence.

1267
01:48:21,970 --> 01:48:24,670
- Is Helen here?
- Yes, sir, she's inside lyin' down,

1268
01:48:24,740 --> 01:48:26,670
tryin' to get
a little sleep.

1269
01:48:26,740 --> 01:48:29,270
We been talkin'
about the appeal, Mr. Finch.

1270
01:48:29,350 --> 01:48:32,210
How long do you think it'll take?

1271
01:48:33,720 --> 01:48:39,210
Spence, there isn't
going to be any appeal.

1272
01:48:40,560 --> 01:48:43,820
Not now.
Tom is dead.

1273
01:48:43,890 --> 01:48:45,830
Dead?

1274
01:48:52,400 --> 01:48:55,960
[ Atticus ]
Helen.

1275
01:48:56,040 --> 01:48:59,100
[ Sobbing ]

1276
01:49:02,910 --> 01:49:07,040
[ Continues Sobbing ]

1277
01:49:24,430 --> 01:49:27,800
Boy.

1278
01:49:27,870 --> 01:49:31,810
Go inside and tell Atticus Finch
I said to come out here.

1279
01:49:31,870 --> 01:49:34,500
Go on, boy.

1280
01:51:12,340 --> 01:51:16,970
[ Adult Scout ] By October,
things had settled down again.

1281
01:51:17,050 --> 01:51:22,810
I still looked for Boo
every time I went by the Radley place.

1282
01:51:24,850 --> 01:51:29,290
This night my mind was filled
with Halloween.

1283
01:51:29,360 --> 01:51:34,130
There was to be a pageant representing
our county's agricultural products.

1284
01:51:34,200 --> 01:51:37,790
I was to be a ham.

1285
01:51:37,870 --> 01:51:43,300
Jem said he would escort me
to the school auditorium.

1286
01:51:43,370 --> 01:51:48,240
Thus began
our longest journey together.

1287
01:52:06,000 --> 01:52:08,760
- Scout!
- [ Scout ] Yeah?

1288
01:52:08,830 --> 01:52:13,100
Will you come on?
Everybody's gone.

1289
01:52:13,170 --> 01:52:15,430
I can't go home like this.

1290
01:52:15,500 --> 01:52:17,940
Well, I'm goin'.
It's almost 10:00...

1291
01:52:18,010 --> 01:52:20,270
and Atticus will be
waiting for us.

1292
01:52:20,340 --> 01:52:23,440
All right, I'm comin'.

1293
01:52:23,510 --> 01:52:27,110
But I feel like a fool
walkin' home like this.

1294
01:52:27,180 --> 01:52:29,450
It's not my fault
you lost your dress.

1295
01:52:29,520 --> 01:52:32,610
I didn't lose it.
Just can't find it.

1296
01:52:32,690 --> 01:52:36,280
- Where are your shoes?
- I can't find them either.

1297
01:52:36,360 --> 01:52:38,950
- You can get 'em tomorrow.
- But tomorrow's Sunday.

1298
01:52:39,030 --> 01:52:42,460
You can get the janitor
to let you in. Come on.

1299
01:53:09,660 --> 01:53:13,590
Here, Scout. Let me hold onto you
before you break your neck.

1300
01:53:17,570 --> 01:53:20,430
- Jem, you don't have to hold me.
- Shh.

1301
01:53:22,340 --> 01:53:25,600
- What's the matter?
- Hush a minute, Scout.

1302
01:53:34,020 --> 01:53:36,450
I thought I heard somethin'.

1303
01:53:38,520 --> 01:53:40,720
Ah... come on.

1304
01:53:51,800 --> 01:53:54,560
Wait.

1305
01:53:54,640 --> 01:53:56,730
- Jem, are you trying to scare me?
- Shh.

1306
01:53:56,810 --> 01:53:59,870
- You know I'm too old.
- Be quiet.

1307
01:53:59,940 --> 01:54:04,240
- [ Dog Howling ]
- I heard an old dog bayin'.

1308
01:54:04,310 --> 01:54:07,410
It's not that. I hear it
when we're walking along.

1309
01:54:07,480 --> 01:54:11,080
When we stop,
I don't hear it anymore.

1310
01:54:11,150 --> 01:54:14,590
Oh, yeah, my costume rustlin'.

1311
01:54:14,660 --> 01:54:17,390
Halloween's got ya.

1312
01:54:17,460 --> 01:54:23,090
[ Rustling ]

1313
01:54:23,170 --> 01:54:26,030
I hear it now.

1314
01:54:26,100 --> 01:54:29,590
I'll bet it's just old Cecil Jacobs
tryin' to scare me.

1315
01:54:29,670 --> 01:54:33,570
Cecil Jacobs
is a big, wet hen!

1316
01:54:37,350 --> 01:54:39,310
[ Rustling ]

1317
01:54:40,680 --> 01:54:42,620
[ Whispers ]
Come on.

1318
01:54:45,350 --> 01:54:49,120
[ Rustling ]

1319
01:54:49,190 --> 01:54:54,720
[ Rustling Continues ]

1320
01:55:13,980 --> 01:55:15,710
- [ Man Shouts ]
- [ Gasps ]

1321
01:55:20,990 --> 01:55:22,920
[ Both Grunting ]

1322
01:55:22,990 --> 01:55:26,620
Run, Scout!
Run, Scout! Run! Run!

1323
01:55:30,670 --> 01:55:35,300
- [ Footsteps, Man Grunting ]
- [ Jem Screams ]

1324
01:55:36,510 --> 01:55:39,300
- [ Jem Gasping ]
- [ Man Grunting ]

1325
01:55:39,370 --> 01:55:44,540
[ Continues Gasping ]

1326
01:55:51,150 --> 01:55:55,820
[ Grunting ]

1327
01:55:57,690 --> 01:55:59,720
[ Screams ]

1328
01:55:59,800 --> 01:56:04,730
[ Men Grunting, Gasping ]

1329
01:56:08,970 --> 01:56:12,910
[ Groaning ]

1330
01:56:14,810 --> 01:56:19,750
[ Grunting, Groaning ]

1331
01:56:19,820 --> 01:56:21,750
[ Fighting Stops ]

1332
01:56:24,990 --> 01:56:26,920
[ Body Falls ]

1333
01:56:32,690 --> 01:56:37,600
[ Footsteps ]

1334
01:56:37,670 --> 01:56:43,130
[ Footsteps Passing ]

1335
01:57:04,790 --> 01:57:06,730
[ Door Creaks Open ]

1336
01:57:14,800 --> 01:57:16,900
Scout! Scout!

1337
01:57:17,970 --> 01:57:20,070
[ Crying ]

1338
01:57:20,140 --> 01:57:22,080
What happened?
What happened?

1339
01:57:22,140 --> 01:57:24,580
I don't know.
I just don't know.

1340
01:57:25,980 --> 01:57:29,110
- Cal, tell Dr. Reynolds to come over.
- Yes, sir.

1341
01:57:30,990 --> 01:57:33,420
- You all right?
- Yes, sir.

1342
01:57:33,490 --> 01:57:35,920
- Are you sure?
- Yes, sir.

1343
01:57:41,700 --> 01:57:43,760
[ Ringing ]

1344
01:57:43,830 --> 01:57:46,360
Sheriff Tate, please.

1345
01:57:46,440 --> 01:57:49,200
Atticus, is Jem dead?

1346
01:57:49,270 --> 01:57:51,870
No. He's unconscious.

1347
01:57:51,940 --> 01:57:55,240
We won't know how badly he's hurt
until the doctor gets here.

1348
01:57:55,310 --> 01:57:57,870
Heck? Atticus Finch.

1349
01:57:57,950 --> 01:58:00,310
Someone's been after my children.

1350
01:58:03,290 --> 01:58:05,720
He's got a bad break.

1351
01:58:05,790 --> 01:58:09,050
Like somebody tried
to wring his arm off.

1352
01:58:15,060 --> 01:58:17,830
I'll be right back, Atticus.

1353
01:58:19,130 --> 01:58:21,070
[ Sheriff ]
How's the boy, Doc?

1354
01:58:21,140 --> 01:58:23,070
He'll be all right.

1355
01:58:25,310 --> 01:58:27,240
Sheriff Tate.

1356
01:58:36,320 --> 01:58:38,310
[ Clears Throat ]

1357
01:58:45,830 --> 01:58:47,760
What is it, Heck?

1358
01:58:52,670 --> 01:58:55,760
Bob Ewell's lyin' on the ground
under that tree down yonder...

1359
01:58:55,840 --> 01:58:59,330
with a kitchen knife
stuck up under his ribs.

1360
01:59:01,480 --> 01:59:03,410
He's dead, Mr. Finch.

1361
01:59:18,030 --> 01:59:21,290
- Are you sure?
- [ Sheriff ] Yes, sir.

1362
01:59:23,270 --> 01:59:27,790
He's not gonna bother
these children anymore.

1363
01:59:29,370 --> 01:59:32,100
Miss Scout, you think you could
tell us what happened?

1364
01:59:33,880 --> 01:59:35,970
I don't know.

1365
01:59:36,040 --> 01:59:38,310
All of a sudden
somebody grabbed me,

1366
01:59:38,380 --> 01:59:40,350
knocked me down
on the ground.

1367
01:59:40,420 --> 01:59:43,150
Jem found me then.

1368
01:59:43,220 --> 01:59:47,520
Then Mr. Ewell, I reckon,
grabbed him again, and Jem hollered.

1369
01:59:47,590 --> 01:59:51,990
Then somebody grabbed me.
Mr. Ewell, I guess.

1370
01:59:52,060 --> 01:59:55,220
Somebody grabbed him...

1371
01:59:55,300 --> 01:59:58,760
and then I heard someone
pantin' and coughin'.

1372
02:00:03,000 --> 02:00:05,170
Then I saw someone
carrying Jem.

1373
02:00:05,240 --> 02:00:07,440
Well, who was it?

1374
02:00:11,510 --> 02:00:13,670
Why, there he is, Mr. Tate.

1375
02:00:13,750 --> 02:00:17,480
He can tell you his name.

1376
02:00:43,710 --> 02:00:45,610
Hey, Boo.

1377
02:00:47,650 --> 02:00:52,250
[ Atticus ] Miss Jean Louise,
Mr. Arthur Radley.

1378
02:00:54,120 --> 02:00:57,060
I believe he already knows you.

1379
02:01:06,500 --> 02:01:08,940
Heck, let's go out
on the front porch.

1380
02:01:21,150 --> 02:01:25,280
Would you like to say good night
to Jem, Mr. Arthur?

1381
02:01:47,640 --> 02:01:51,410
You can pet him, Mr. Arthur.
He's asleep.

1382
02:01:51,480 --> 02:01:54,940
You couldn't if he was awake, though.
He wouldn't let you.

1383
02:01:58,820 --> 02:02:01,080
Go ahead.

1384
02:02:38,690 --> 02:02:41,790
[ Scout ]
Let's go set in the swing, Mr. Arthur.

1385
02:02:45,800 --> 02:02:48,740
[ Atticus ]
Heck, l-l--

1386
02:02:48,800 --> 02:02:54,070
I guess that
the thing to do is, uh--

1387
02:02:56,980 --> 02:03:01,750
Good Lord...
I must be losin' my memory.

1388
02:03:03,150 --> 02:03:08,750
I can't remember
whether Jem is 12 or 13.

1389
02:03:11,830 --> 02:03:17,590
Anyway, it'll have to come
before the county court.

1390
02:03:20,500 --> 02:03:24,440
Of course, it's a clear-cut case
of self-defense.

1391
02:03:24,510 --> 02:03:28,100
I'll, uh-- Well, I'll run down
to the office--

1392
02:03:28,180 --> 02:03:30,770
Mr. Finch...

1393
02:03:30,850 --> 02:03:33,440
do you think Jem
killed Bob Ewell?

1394
02:03:33,520 --> 02:03:36,780
Is that what you think?

1395
02:03:36,850 --> 02:03:40,120
Your boy never stabbed him.

1396
02:03:56,970 --> 02:04:00,570
Bob Ewell fell on his knife.

1397
02:04:00,640 --> 02:04:04,080
He killed himself.

1398
02:04:07,480 --> 02:04:10,420
There's a black man
dead for no reason.

1399
02:04:10,490 --> 02:04:14,420
Now the man
responsible for it is dead.

1400
02:04:14,490 --> 02:04:17,580
Let the dead bury the dead
this time, Mr. Finch.

1401
02:04:17,660 --> 02:04:20,090
I never heard tell it was
against the law for any citizen...

1402
02:04:20,160 --> 02:04:22,760
to do his utmost to prevent a crime
from being committed,

1403
02:04:22,830 --> 02:04:24,920
which is exactly what he did.

1404
02:04:25,000 --> 02:04:30,600
But maybe you'll tell me
it's my duty to tell the town
all about it, not to hush it up.

1405
02:04:30,670 --> 02:04:33,110
You know what'll happen then.

1406
02:04:33,170 --> 02:04:35,440
All the ladies in Maycomb,
includin' my wife,

1407
02:04:35,510 --> 02:04:39,950
will be knockin' on his door
bringin' angel food cakes.

1408
02:04:40,020 --> 02:04:43,280
To my way of thinkin',
takin' one man,

1409
02:04:43,350 --> 02:04:46,180
who done you and this town
a big service,

1410
02:04:46,250 --> 02:04:50,380
and draggin' him with his shy ways
into the limelight--

1411
02:04:50,460 --> 02:04:53,720
To me, that's a sin.

1412
02:04:53,800 --> 02:04:55,890
It's a sin.

1413
02:04:55,960 --> 02:04:59,900
And I'm not about
to have it on my head.

1414
02:04:59,970 --> 02:05:02,900
I may not be much,
Mr. Finch,

1415
02:05:02,970 --> 02:05:06,570
but I'm still Sheriff
of Maycomb County,

1416
02:05:06,640 --> 02:05:10,740
and Bob Ewell fell on his knife.

1417
02:05:10,810 --> 02:05:12,750
Good night, sir.

1418
02:05:18,150 --> 02:05:22,590
[ Car Door Shuts, Engine Starts ]

1419
02:05:51,950 --> 02:05:54,550
Mr. Tate was right.

1420
02:05:58,630 --> 02:06:01,220
What do you mean?

1421
02:06:01,300 --> 02:06:05,060
Well...
it would be...

1422
02:06:05,130 --> 02:06:09,400
sort of like shooting
a mockingbird, wouldn't it?

1423
02:06:37,500 --> 02:06:39,590
Thank you, Arthur.

1424
02:06:47,440 --> 02:06:50,380
Thank you for my children.

1425
02:07:04,630 --> 02:07:07,890
[ Adult Scout ]
Neighbors bring food with death...

1426
02:07:07,960 --> 02:07:10,060
and flowers with sickness...

1427
02:07:10,130 --> 02:07:13,730
and little things in between.

1428
02:07:13,800 --> 02:07:16,570
Boo was our neighbor.

1429
02:07:16,640 --> 02:07:19,400
He gave us two soap dolls,

1430
02:07:19,470 --> 02:07:22,240
a broken watch and chain,

1431
02:07:22,310 --> 02:07:24,570
a knife...

1432
02:07:24,650 --> 02:07:27,080
and our lives.

1433
02:07:31,990 --> 02:07:34,920
One time Atticus said...

1434
02:07:34,990 --> 02:07:40,430
you never really knew a man
until you stood in his shoes
and walked around in them.

1435
02:07:45,000 --> 02:07:49,200
Just standing
on the Radley porch was enough.

1436
02:07:55,780 --> 02:07:59,540
The summer that had begun
so long ago had ended,

1437
02:07:59,610 --> 02:08:02,050
and another summer
had taken its place.

1438
02:08:02,120 --> 02:08:04,050
And a fall.

1439
02:08:04,120 --> 02:08:07,550
And Boo Radley had come out.

1440
02:08:10,790 --> 02:08:14,230
I was to think
of these days many times,

1441
02:08:14,300 --> 02:08:16,390
of Jem and Dill...

1442
02:08:16,460 --> 02:08:19,730
and Boo Radley
and Tom Robinson.

1443
02:08:21,470 --> 02:08:23,730
And Atticus.

1444
02:08:31,480 --> 02:08:35,580
He would be
in Jem's room all night,

1445
02:08:35,650 --> 02:08:40,590
and he would be there when Jem
waked up in the morning.

